WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.390 --> 00:00:09.420
- Good afternoon, hello everyone.

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:11.250
My name is Dean Timeica Bethel,

00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:13.290
I am Assistant Dean of Yale College

00:00:13.290 --> 00:00:16.110
and director of the
Afro-American Cultural Center,

00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:17.970
more affectionately known as The House,

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:19.590
AKA, this big beautiful building

00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:21.300
you're sitting in right now.

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:23.280
So welcome home.

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:24.651
Before we begin now,

00:00:24.651 --> 00:00:27.240
I would like to do the
land acknowledgement.

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:29.130
So Yale University acknowledges

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:31.230
that indigenous peoples and nations,

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:34.350
including the Mohegan,
the Mashantucket Pequot,

00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:36.900
the Eastern Pequot, the Schaghticoke,

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:39.810
the Golden Hill Paugussett, the Niantic,

00:00:39.810 --> 00:00:43.110
and the Quinnipiac, and other
Algonquian speaking peoples

00:00:43.110 --> 00:00:46.710
have stewarded through
generations the land and waterways

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:48.390
of what is now the State of Connecticut

00:00:48.390 --> 00:00:50.610
and where our university sits.

00:00:50.610 --> 00:00:53.040
We honor and respect the
enduring relationship

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:56.253
that exists between these peoples
and nations and this land.

00:00:57.450 --> 00:00:59.010
I would like to briefly tell you a bit

00:00:59.010 --> 00:01:01.980
about The House before
our event begins today.

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:06.030
So the Afro-American Cultural
Center was founded in 1969

00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:07.650
by three Yale undergraduates,

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:10.800
Don Ogilvie, Armstead
Robinson, and Glenn de Chabert.

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:13.620
They worked for two years
with local New Haven activists

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:15.060
and some professors on campus

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:17.280
to petition the University for a space

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:18.960
for Black students to call home

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:20.580
while they were here at Yale.

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:23.760
Luckily, smartly, the Yale Corporation

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:25.650
approved their petition,

00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:28.200
and we were granted our center in 1969.

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:30.600
We moved into this building in 1971,

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:32.340
and we've been here ever since.

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:35.490
The House is home to over
50 student organizations,

00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:38.580
both undergraduates, and graduate
and professional students

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:41.190
who are able to use our
space free of charge,

00:01:41.190 --> 00:01:43.890
have their events here, get resources,

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:45.960
and get guidance from
our professional staff,

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:48.441
which as of right now it's just me.

00:01:48.441 --> 00:01:51.990
Along with the 54-year
rich history of The House,

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:54.300
The House continues to be
mostly run by students.

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:58.320
So we have over 50 students
staff here and myself

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:00.450
with over 2,000 Black undergrads

00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:02.253
and GMP students here at Yale.

00:02:03.772 --> 00:02:04.605
So it's very exciting,

00:02:04.605 --> 00:02:05.910
especially as an alum of Yale College

00:02:05.910 --> 00:02:08.790
from the class of 2011
to be able to be here

00:02:08.790 --> 00:02:12.030
to steward Yalies through this
space and this experience.

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:13.017
I welcome all of you,

00:02:13.017 --> 00:02:15.420
and I encourage you to explore this space

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:16.980
once this event is over.

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:19.890
It is the first cultural
center on Yale's campus,

00:02:19.890 --> 00:02:21.960
the first Black cultural
center of the Ivy League,

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:24.690
and one of the first Black
cultural centers in the country.

00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:27.030
It is also the largest and, in my opinion,

00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:29.550
the best building on this campus,

00:02:29.550 --> 00:02:31.350
the best cultural center on this campus,

00:02:31.350 --> 00:02:33.840
so please be sure to explore our building.

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:37.020
We have a library, art gallery,
meeting spaces and things,

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:39.330
and we are very excited
to have all of you here

00:02:39.330 --> 00:02:41.520
for this wonderful, and very purposeful,

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:43.350
and timely event today.

00:02:43.350 --> 00:02:46.000
And with that, I will pass
you off to Dean Imani.

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:49.167
(audience applauding)

00:02:51.779 --> 00:02:53.197
Sorry.
- Sorry.

00:02:53.197 --> 00:02:55.740
Thank you very much,
and thank you very much

00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:58.590
for allowing us to host our event here

00:02:58.590 --> 00:03:00.990
in this beautiful, beautiful
(indistinct) house,

00:03:02.277 --> 00:03:05.850
I really feel honored to
be in this place today.

00:03:05.850 --> 00:03:08.790
And I would like to
just welcome all of you,

00:03:08.790 --> 00:03:12.363
good afternoon and thank you
very much for joining us today.

00:03:13.479 --> 00:03:14.312
(object crackling* on the floor)

00:03:14.312 --> 00:03:16.500
We're all, oh sorry (chuckles).

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:19.350
We're all so pleased to welcome

00:03:19.350 --> 00:03:24.000
award-winning filmmaker Tonya Lewis Lee,

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:27.720
who is here with us in
this beautiful House

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:31.230
for this special centennial conversation.

00:03:31.230 --> 00:03:33.240
As you know that we are celebrating

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:34.680
our centennial this year,

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:37.680
and this is a part of a
series of wonderful events

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:40.263
that we are going to hold
throughout this year.

00:03:41.370 --> 00:03:43.410
I know we all look forward

00:03:43.410 --> 00:03:47.520
to hearing about her
documentary film, "After Shock",

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:52.290
I hope that you have all watched
it, and her important work

00:03:52.290 --> 00:03:57.290
in the areas of maternal
mortality and social justice.

00:03:57.330 --> 00:04:00.360
These are two areas that
we care so much about

00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:01.980
in the Yale School of Nursing,

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:05.880
and we can't wait to hear
the conversation here,

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:10.880
and to learn more about both areas,

00:04:11.970 --> 00:04:15.510
and to see what could
we do as an institution

00:04:15.510 --> 00:04:20.510
to be able to promote,
you know, social justice,

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:24.870
and health equity, and health justice

00:04:24.870 --> 00:04:27.196
across the United States.

00:04:27.196 --> 00:04:31.080
I would also would like to welcome

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:35.940
four of our precious Yale
School of Nursing faculty

00:04:35.940 --> 00:04:39.453
who are going to participate
this conversation today.

00:04:40.298 --> 00:04:45.298
Midwife and student equity
coordinator, Heather Reynolds,

00:04:46.710 --> 00:04:50.010
nurse midwifery specialty co-directors,

00:04:50.010 --> 00:04:54.900
Joan Combellick and Michelle Telfer.

00:04:54.900 --> 00:04:59.719
And also last but not least,
our former interim dean

00:04:59.719 --> 00:05:03.660
of Yale School of Nursing,
Holly Powell Kennedy,

00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:06.908
and Helen Varney Professor of Midwifery.

00:05:06.908 --> 00:05:10.075
(audience applauding)

00:05:12.030 --> 00:05:14.070
I also would like to use this opportunity

00:05:14.070 --> 00:05:16.950
to acknowledge and thank the many people

00:05:16.950 --> 00:05:20.850
whose efforts made today's event possible,

00:05:20.850 --> 00:05:25.850
including Tonys's team,
Michelle Koss, Mike Regan.

00:05:27.480 --> 00:05:29.040
I think that some of you are here,

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:31.953
so if you are here, just
wave so that we can see you.

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:38.240
Taylor Testone, Peter
Plotica, Lauren Langford,

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:43.620
and Marsha Thomas, here.

00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:44.550
Thank you very much.

00:05:44.550 --> 00:05:48.880
And now it's my real pleasure
to welcome Heather Reynolds

00:05:48.880 --> 00:05:53.156
to the podium for introducing Tonya.

00:05:53.156 --> 00:05:55.915
(audience applauding)

00:05:55.915 --> 00:05:58.998
(indistinct chatter)

00:06:00.239 --> 00:06:03.539
- Oh my gosh, it's so good
to be back in this place.

00:06:03.539 --> 00:06:04.372
(audience laughing)

00:06:04.372 --> 00:06:06.120
I was here as a student back in the '70s,

00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:07.983
so for me, this is like a homecoming.

00:06:08.931 --> 00:06:12.870
I just want to say that
this incredible lady,

00:06:12.870 --> 00:06:17.010
Tonya Lewis Lee is a
producer, film director,

00:06:17.010 --> 00:06:20.612
writer, entrepreneur, a
woman's health advocate,

00:06:20.612 --> 00:06:25.337
who for over 20 years has been
delivering meaningful content

00:06:26.220 --> 00:06:28.533
that resonates with
marginalized community.

00:06:29.430 --> 00:06:31.023
As a television producer,

00:06:31.860 --> 00:06:34.897
Lee served as executive
producer on the Netflix series,

00:06:34.897 --> 00:06:36.630
"She's Gotta Have It,"

00:06:36.630 --> 00:06:40.110
it was a movie before
and then became a series.

00:06:40.110 --> 00:06:41.947
She produced her first limited Series,

00:06:41.947 --> 00:06:45.180
"Miracle's Boys" at Nickelodeon

00:06:45.180 --> 00:06:48.300
based on the Jacqueline
Woodson novel of the same name,

00:06:48.300 --> 00:06:50.223
which remains a classic today.

00:06:51.364 --> 00:06:53.700
As a film producer, Lee produced

00:06:53.700 --> 00:06:57.780
the adaptation of the Walter
Dean Myers novel "Monster,"

00:06:57.780 --> 00:06:59.340
which premiered in competition

00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:02.670
at the 2018 Sundance Film Festival

00:07:02.670 --> 00:07:05.190
and is streaming on Netflix.

00:07:05.190 --> 00:07:07.320
Most recently, Lee co-directed

00:07:07.320 --> 00:07:09.630
and co-produced the Emmy nominated,

00:07:09.630 --> 00:07:11.730
Peabody Award-Winning "After Shock,"

00:07:11.730 --> 00:07:14.130
which we're gonna be celebrating today,

00:07:14.130 --> 00:07:18.420
which examines the US
maternal mortality crisis.

00:07:18.420 --> 00:07:19.830
The documentary premiered

00:07:19.830 --> 00:07:22.560
at the 2020 Sundance Film Festival,

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:26.640
and won the Special Jury
Award for Impact for Change.

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:30.660
It was acquired by Onyx and
ABC News to release on Hulu,

00:07:30.660 --> 00:07:34.432
in 20 July of last year.

00:07:34.432 --> 00:07:37.680
As a writer, Tonya penned the script

00:07:37.680 --> 00:07:40.140
for "The Watsons Go to Birmingham,"

00:07:40.140 --> 00:07:43.590
an adaptation of the
Christopher Paul Curtis novel

00:07:43.590 --> 00:07:46.890
that she also produced
for the Hallmark Channel.

00:07:46.890 --> 00:07:49.050
She's the author of
three children's books,

00:07:49.050 --> 00:07:52.380
including the classic,
"Please, Baby Please,"

00:07:52.380 --> 00:07:56.070
as well as the adult
novel "Gotham Diaries."

00:07:56.070 --> 00:07:57.780
She has done a lot (chuckles).

00:07:57.780 --> 00:08:01.380
We're almost there 'cause
we want to hear from her.

00:08:01.380 --> 00:08:04.650
In 2007, Tonya became the spokesperson for

00:08:04.650 --> 00:08:08.070
the US Department of Health
and Human Services Office

00:08:08.070 --> 00:08:12.510
of Minority Health Infant
Mortality Awareness Campaign,

00:08:12.510 --> 00:08:14.880
a Healthy Baby Begins With You,

00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:16.800
which ignited her lifelong passion

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:18.780
for advocating for better health outcomes

00:08:18.780 --> 00:08:20.910
for all women in the United States,

00:08:20.910 --> 00:08:23.043
especially women of color.

00:08:24.540 --> 00:08:26.940
Through the campaign, Tonya produced

00:08:26.940 --> 00:08:30.330
the film "Crisis in the Crib:
Saving Our Nation's Babies"

00:08:30.330 --> 00:08:32.670
'cause it's not just maternal mortality,

00:08:32.670 --> 00:08:34.050
but infant mortality too,

00:08:34.050 --> 00:08:35.973
which is unfortunately on the rise.

00:08:37.110 --> 00:08:39.420
This work led Tonya to
embark on her journey

00:08:39.420 --> 00:08:43.020
as an entrepreneur to
create Movita Organics,

00:08:43.020 --> 00:08:45.600
which is an organic
vitamin supplement brand

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:48.150
to continue the conversation with women

00:08:48.150 --> 00:08:50.670
about accessing healthy outcomes,

00:08:50.670 --> 00:08:52.710
and provide them with
a high quality resource

00:08:52.710 --> 00:08:55.860
to aid their wellbeing
and overall vitality.

00:08:55.860 --> 00:08:58.080
She's a member of the
Writer's Guild of America

00:08:58.080 --> 00:09:00.510
and a member of the
Producer's Guild of America,

00:09:00.510 --> 00:09:03.510
serving as co-chair of
the One Guide Committee

00:09:03.510 --> 00:09:06.510
and on the Producer's
Council Board of Delegates.

00:09:06.510 --> 00:09:08.760
She's also a member of
the Board of Trustees

00:09:08.760 --> 00:09:09.858
of the March of Dimes,

00:09:09.858 --> 00:09:12.420
where I'm also on their advisory council,

00:09:12.420 --> 00:09:15.900
the Nurse's Advisory Council,
and the director emeritus

00:09:15.900 --> 00:09:19.500
of the board of Trustees
of the NAACP Legal Defense

00:09:19.500 --> 00:09:20.913
and Educational Fund.

00:09:21.990 --> 00:09:25.140
In 2022, IndieWire featured Tonya

00:09:25.140 --> 00:09:29.520
in their 22 rising female
filmmakers to watch.

00:09:29.520 --> 00:09:31.350
She's the mother of two adult children,

00:09:31.350 --> 00:09:34.290
and lives in New York with
her husband Spike Lee.

00:09:34.290 --> 00:09:37.519
Welcome, it's a pleasure to
have you here, (indistinct).

00:09:37.519 --> 00:09:40.686
(audience applauding)

00:09:45.138 --> 00:09:47.700
(gentle music)

00:09:47.700 --> 00:09:49.530
(tape rolling)

00:09:49.530 --> 00:09:53.310
- My daughter's story is
loud, colorful, and artful.

00:09:53.310 --> 00:09:54.310
- [All] It's a girl!

00:09:55.200 --> 00:09:58.080
- She was awake, aware, and active.

00:09:58.080 --> 00:09:58.953
Hi baby.

00:10:00.600 --> 00:10:02.868
And yet she still died.

00:10:02.868 --> 00:10:04.410
(suspenseful music)

00:10:04.410 --> 00:10:05.880
- [Interviewee] After she gave birth,

00:10:05.880 --> 00:10:07.050
Shamony was complaining

00:10:07.050 --> 00:10:09.480
that she had really sharp chest pains.

00:10:09.480 --> 00:10:10.950
- [Friend] The ambulance came,

00:10:10.950 --> 00:10:12.540
I'm telling them the symptoms.

00:10:12.540 --> 00:10:13.590
- [Nurse] Is she on drugs?

00:10:13.590 --> 00:10:16.020
- Next set of people come
in, "Is she on drugs?"

00:10:16.020 --> 00:10:18.346
- They kept asking her
mother, "Is she on any drug?"

00:10:18.346 --> 00:10:20.040
- (chuckles) I'm like, "Do y'all talk?"

00:10:20.040 --> 00:10:23.216
- We waited, a solid 12 hours.

00:10:23.216 --> 00:10:24.720
(machine beeping)

00:10:24.720 --> 00:10:26.458
- [Shamony] She's gone.

00:10:26.458 --> 00:10:29.178
(dramatic percussion)

00:10:29.178 --> 00:10:30.330
- Black women are four
times more likely to die

00:10:30.330 --> 00:10:33.240
than their white counterparts
with the same symptoms,

00:10:33.240 --> 00:10:34.203
why is that?

00:10:35.260 --> 00:10:37.860
- This is a growing
epidemic in our community.

00:10:37.860 --> 00:10:39.240
- Hundreds and thousands of men

00:10:39.240 --> 00:10:42.075
are going through this same situation.

00:10:42.075 --> 00:10:44.185
(suspenseful music)

00:10:44.185 --> 00:10:47.550
♪ Well, you couldn't tell me
that I wouldn't be there. ♪

00:10:47.550 --> 00:10:49.893
- I've never lived in
this house without her.

00:10:50.730 --> 00:10:52.530
- You just gotta keep pushing forward.

00:10:52.530 --> 00:10:54.663
I can't let Amber be another statistic.

00:10:55.623 --> 00:10:57.842
♪ I had a plan ♪

00:10:57.842 --> 00:10:58.800
♪ I had it lived out ♪

00:10:58.800 --> 00:11:00.150
- If these numbers were flipped around,

00:11:00.150 --> 00:11:01.230
and white women were dying

00:11:01.230 --> 00:11:02.550
at the rate the Black women dying,

00:11:02.550 --> 00:11:04.613
it would be a national crisis.

00:11:04.613 --> 00:11:06.240
- [Speaker] We fight
against maternal morbidity,

00:11:06.240 --> 00:11:09.810
event by event in order to create change.

00:11:09.810 --> 00:11:11.250
- We can turn our pain into power,

00:11:11.250 --> 00:11:12.780
and make something of this.

00:11:12.780 --> 00:11:17.320
- Shamony Gibson, we hear you, we see you.

00:11:18.161 --> 00:11:19.890
I planned on spending
a lifetime with Amber,

00:11:19.890 --> 00:11:21.630
I wanted to give her my life.

00:11:21.630 --> 00:11:23.951
This way, I am still going to.

00:11:23.951 --> 00:11:27.534
♪ Sometimes change ♪

00:11:27.534 --> 00:11:28.367
- [Megaphone Speaker] The revolution

00:11:28.367 --> 00:11:31.020
will be Tweeted, Instagrammed, Facebooked.

00:11:31.020 --> 00:11:32.604
- This fight is not over.

00:11:32.604 --> 00:11:33.630
- [Megaphone Speaker]
When Black mothers die,

00:11:33.630 --> 00:11:34.830
there's a ripple effect.

00:11:37.770 --> 00:11:39.820
We call it "Aftershocks."

00:11:39.820 --> 00:11:43.320
♪ Things sometimes change ♪

00:11:45.693 --> 00:11:48.728
- Hi everybody, thank you for being here.

00:11:48.728 --> 00:11:52.170
First, thank you so much to all of you all

00:11:52.170 --> 00:11:53.640
who brought me here.

00:11:53.640 --> 00:11:57.000
I am truly honored and humbled to be here

00:11:57.000 --> 00:11:59.220
to talk to you today about "Aftershock",

00:11:59.220 --> 00:12:02.580
and to be in conversation
with these amazing women here.

00:12:03.966 --> 00:12:05.970
I'm gonna tell you a little
bit about how I got here

00:12:05.970 --> 00:12:08.280
before we start the conversation,

00:12:08.280 --> 00:12:10.996
and why I've been so passionate

00:12:10.996 --> 00:12:15.030
about the work of maternal health,

00:12:15.030 --> 00:12:18.900
and equalizing the
maternal healthcare here.

00:12:18.900 --> 00:12:20.220
I think it's kind of obvious,

00:12:20.220 --> 00:12:22.859
but I will still talk about it.

00:12:22.859 --> 00:12:27.313
You know, one of the things
you didn't mention in that bio

00:12:28.650 --> 00:12:30.183
is that I'm a lawyer by trade.

00:12:32.112 --> 00:12:35.340
When I graduated from
college as a young person,

00:12:35.340 --> 00:12:38.193
I actually really wanted
to be in the arts.

00:12:39.150 --> 00:12:42.180
I was someone who had
studied a lot of literature,

00:12:42.180 --> 00:12:46.110
I went to Sarah Lawrence
College, a very creative place.

00:12:46.110 --> 00:12:47.620
I did a lot of writing

00:12:48.600 --> 00:12:53.600
but I didn't know anyone who
had a career as a creative.

00:12:53.700 --> 00:12:55.620
My parents were very corporate minded,

00:12:55.620 --> 00:12:56.520
and they didn't either.

00:12:56.520 --> 00:12:58.320
And when I said I wanted
to go into television,

00:12:58.320 --> 00:12:59.903
they were like, "Are you crazy?"

00:12:59.903 --> 00:13:01.651
(audience laughing)

00:13:01.651 --> 00:13:02.484
(chuckles) "You need to get a job,

00:13:02.484 --> 00:13:03.387
"so you can make some money."

00:13:05.441 --> 00:13:07.380
And so I went to law
school thinking I would,

00:13:07.380 --> 00:13:09.660
at one point thinking I would
be a civil rights lawyer.

00:13:09.660 --> 00:13:11.587
But then again, my father was like,

00:13:11.587 --> 00:13:15.079
"That's not what I meant when
I sent you to law school.

00:13:15.079 --> 00:13:16.530
"I want you to make some money."

00:13:16.530 --> 00:13:17.880
So I ended up being a corporate lawyer,

00:13:17.880 --> 00:13:21.603
although I did clerk at the
NAACP Legal Defense Fund.

00:13:22.470 --> 00:13:24.450
And I think my passion,

00:13:24.450 --> 00:13:27.570
I was just talking to someone
about this the other day

00:13:27.570 --> 00:13:30.090
for civil rights and social justice,

00:13:30.090 --> 00:13:33.330
when I was eight years old
in the middle of third grade,

00:13:33.330 --> 00:13:37.053
we moved from Montclair, New
Jersey to Milwaukee, Wisconsin.

00:13:37.957 --> 00:13:42.120
And it was a traumatic
move for me in a way,

00:13:42.120 --> 00:13:44.550
my father's company moved, so we moved.

00:13:44.550 --> 00:13:46.867
And back in that day,
my parents were like,

00:13:46.867 --> 00:13:49.020
"Yeah, when dad's going, we all go,"

00:13:49.020 --> 00:13:51.438
which is great in some ways.

00:13:51.438 --> 00:13:53.130
But moving to Milwaukee,

00:13:53.130 --> 00:13:55.710
the suburbs of Milwaukee, Wisconsin,

00:13:55.710 --> 00:13:59.100
I was like the second Black
student in that school.

00:13:59.100 --> 00:14:03.060
And it was a very racist place,

00:14:03.060 --> 00:14:08.040
and I was an 8-year-old child
being bullied by the kids,

00:14:08.040 --> 00:14:13.040
not protected by the teachers
there, and felt very alone.

00:14:13.290 --> 00:14:14.880
And at the end of the year, that year,

00:14:14.880 --> 00:14:18.330
my parents transferred
me to another school.

00:14:18.330 --> 00:14:21.950
But I bring that up because
what I think I'm trying to do

00:14:21.950 --> 00:14:23.040
in the work that I'm doing

00:14:23.040 --> 00:14:25.983
is fight for that little
8-year-old girl (chuckles).

00:14:25.983 --> 00:14:28.590
I think my work is about

00:14:28.590 --> 00:14:30.990
trying to make the world a better place,

00:14:30.990 --> 00:14:33.459
so that other young people
don't have to go through that.

00:14:33.459 --> 00:14:37.410
At the same time, I remember very clearly

00:14:37.410 --> 00:14:42.360
watching the documentary
series, "Eyes on the Prize,"

00:14:42.360 --> 00:14:44.490
these things collide to me in my mind

00:14:44.490 --> 00:14:45.819
as an adult now, right?

00:14:45.819 --> 00:14:50.130
"Eyes on the Prize"
was eye-opening for me,

00:14:50.130 --> 00:14:53.940
there were these amazing civil
rights warriors out there,

00:14:53.940 --> 00:14:57.030
fighting that fight for
all of us to have rights.

00:14:57.030 --> 00:14:59.310
I mean, young people standing up

00:14:59.310 --> 00:15:02.400
against dogs and water hoses,

00:15:02.400 --> 00:15:07.020
and I think that really got
into my spirit and my soul.

00:15:07.020 --> 00:15:11.940
And so, you know, while I
was looking to make my way

00:15:11.940 --> 00:15:14.730
in life in some way using the things,

00:15:14.730 --> 00:15:17.160
I was an early reader, I love stories,

00:15:17.160 --> 00:15:20.850
I love watching movies,
I love watching TV.

00:15:20.850 --> 00:15:24.570
And ultimately, somehow I
wanted to bring it all together

00:15:24.570 --> 00:15:27.450
and you know, it's funny,

00:15:27.450 --> 00:15:29.284
all of my friends were always artists.

00:15:29.284 --> 00:15:31.711
And then I happened to marry

00:15:31.711 --> 00:15:35.541
a super creative,
amazing artist (chuckles)

00:15:35.541 --> 00:15:36.374
(audience laughing)

00:15:36.374 --> 00:15:37.980
who was making it work for himself, right?

00:15:37.980 --> 00:15:40.590
And when we got married, you know,

00:15:40.590 --> 00:15:42.150
I talked to Spike about the fact that

00:15:42.150 --> 00:15:44.640
I really had these creative ideas.

00:15:44.640 --> 00:15:46.027
And he was like, "If
that's what you wanna do,

00:15:46.027 --> 00:15:47.550
"then you should go do that,"

00:15:47.550 --> 00:15:49.590
and really supported me in doing that.

00:15:49.590 --> 00:15:53.100
And I started by writing, you know,

00:15:53.100 --> 00:15:55.413
I was newly married, I gave up my job,

00:15:56.499 --> 00:15:59.199
and I'd get up in the morning
and I'd write for hours.

00:16:00.150 --> 00:16:02.257
And that resulted in ultimately

00:16:02.257 --> 00:16:06.270
my first children's book,
"Please Baby, Please."

00:16:06.270 --> 00:16:07.950
And because of "Please Baby, Please,"

00:16:07.950 --> 00:16:09.930
and then "Please Puppy, Please,"

00:16:09.930 --> 00:16:12.450
the US Department of
Health and Human Services

00:16:12.450 --> 00:16:15.930
reached out to me and asked
me to be a spokesperson

00:16:15.930 --> 00:16:18.900
for the Infant Mortality
Awareness Raising Campaign,

00:16:18.900 --> 00:16:21.330
A Healthy Baby Begins With You.

00:16:21.330 --> 00:16:25.710
And with that campaign I had the absolute,

00:16:25.710 --> 00:16:28.950
I mean, blessed experience
to travel this country,

00:16:28.950 --> 00:16:30.441
all over this country.

00:16:30.441 --> 00:16:34.860
I was in urban areas,
I was in rural areas,

00:16:34.860 --> 00:16:39.860
north, south, east, west, and
got a chance to meet people

00:16:42.120 --> 00:16:44.202
where they were, the community,

00:16:44.202 --> 00:16:48.150
people who were working in community

00:16:48.150 --> 00:16:50.640
to try to improve outcomes.

00:16:50.640 --> 00:16:53.400
And I got a major education,
I mean at the time,

00:16:53.400 --> 00:16:55.432
I don't even think I at first knew

00:16:55.432 --> 00:16:59.430
what infant mortality was,
that the US had an issue,

00:16:59.430 --> 00:17:00.780
that Black babies were dying

00:17:00.780 --> 00:17:02.910
at higher rates than white babies.

00:17:02.910 --> 00:17:04.020
That it didn't matter

00:17:04.020 --> 00:17:06.720
your socioeconomic status in this country,

00:17:06.720 --> 00:17:09.240
as a Black person, you
still had a higher chance

00:17:09.240 --> 00:17:12.300
of losing your child
before its first birthday.

00:17:12.300 --> 00:17:15.962
And finding myself immersed in this world

00:17:15.962 --> 00:17:17.580
of ultimately women's health,

00:17:17.580 --> 00:17:19.230
because, of course, when
we talk about an infant,

00:17:19.230 --> 00:17:21.080
we're talking about a woman's health.

00:17:22.110 --> 00:17:27.110
I would inevitably hear stories,
not only of infant death,

00:17:27.750 --> 00:17:32.146
but of mothers dying from
childbirth complications.

00:17:32.146 --> 00:17:33.990
And so, as Heather mentioned,

00:17:33.990 --> 00:17:36.420
I did a film called
Crisis in the Crib then

00:17:36.420 --> 00:17:38.760
for the Office of Minority Health.

00:17:38.760 --> 00:17:41.190
And I went on and
continued to do other work,

00:17:41.190 --> 00:17:42.930
continued to travel with the campaign

00:17:42.930 --> 00:17:46.380
because I think once your
eyes are opened in this way,

00:17:46.380 --> 00:17:50.645
once you have the opportunity
to really get out in community

00:17:50.645 --> 00:17:54.810
and engage with people, it
just becomes a part of you.

00:17:54.810 --> 00:17:55.770
It certainly did for me.

00:17:55.770 --> 00:17:58.740
I mean, I love going into community

00:17:58.740 --> 00:18:00.750
and talking to all kinds of people.

00:18:00.750 --> 00:18:03.120
I mean, it just fills, it fills me.

00:18:03.120 --> 00:18:05.460
And I feel like I take them with me

00:18:05.460 --> 00:18:08.733
and I can share them in other ways.

00:18:10.560 --> 00:18:15.120
But then, you know, in
terms of "Aftershock" in,

00:18:15.120 --> 00:18:19.951
I think it was 2018, Linda
wrote, Linda Villarosa

00:18:19.951 --> 00:18:24.510
wrote a huge article
for the "New York Times"

00:18:24.510 --> 00:18:27.480
about the US maternal mortality crisis.

00:18:27.480 --> 00:18:30.150
And I think ProPublica did an article

00:18:30.150 --> 00:18:32.010
and people were starting to talk about

00:18:32.010 --> 00:18:35.670
what was happening in our
country around maternal health.

00:18:35.670 --> 00:18:38.100
And I thought to myself,
"I better hurry up

00:18:38.100 --> 00:18:40.167
and make a documentary about this,

00:18:40.167 --> 00:18:42.600
'cause I wanna give my
my point of view on it

00:18:42.600 --> 00:18:43.920
before somebody else does."

00:18:43.920 --> 00:18:45.630
'Cause I'm gonna be really
mad if somebody else

00:18:45.630 --> 00:18:47.550
does and they don't do it right.

00:18:47.550 --> 00:18:52.550
So, you know, I started thinking about it

00:18:53.130 --> 00:18:54.960
and it's a huge issue.

00:18:54.960 --> 00:18:57.420
And I say that, I joke and say I'd be mad

00:18:57.420 --> 00:18:58.680
if someone didn't do it right.

00:18:58.680 --> 00:19:00.570
But the fact of the matter is,

00:19:00.570 --> 00:19:02.730
and I know there are other
people working on documentaries,

00:19:02.730 --> 00:19:04.830
other documentaries have
come out on the issue.

00:19:04.830 --> 00:19:06.135
And I think it's really important

00:19:06.135 --> 00:19:07.700
that people put their voice

00:19:07.700 --> 00:19:10.380
to the issue the way they see it.

00:19:10.380 --> 00:19:14.010
And for me, it was
really important to work

00:19:14.010 --> 00:19:15.540
with somebody, to find a partner.

00:19:15.540 --> 00:19:17.910
Because what way, there's
so many different ways in

00:19:17.910 --> 00:19:20.790
that you can go about making this film.

00:19:20.790 --> 00:19:24.597
And so my producing directing
partner, Paula Eiselt

00:19:24.597 --> 00:19:26.793
and I met at a women's conference.

00:19:27.690 --> 00:19:30.000
Someone introduced us, they
mentioned that she had,

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:33.570
was beginning to develop a
documentary film on the issue.

00:19:33.570 --> 00:19:34.860
I was beginning to develop a

00:19:34.860 --> 00:19:36.300
documentary of film on the issue.

00:19:36.300 --> 00:19:38.400
We did not know each other.

00:19:38.400 --> 00:19:40.740
She's a Jewish woman
who lives in New Jersey.

00:19:40.740 --> 00:19:42.690
I am who I am.

00:19:42.690 --> 00:19:45.930
We met one day, we had coffee another day.

00:19:45.930 --> 00:19:49.200
And the next thing you know,
we were off making this film.

00:19:49.200 --> 00:19:52.170
And the reason why we were off
to make this film together is

00:19:52.170 --> 00:19:55.620
that we both had a shared
vision about how this film,

00:19:55.620 --> 00:19:58.290
from our perspective needed to be told.

00:19:58.290 --> 00:20:02.220
And that was centering the women

00:20:02.220 --> 00:20:04.620
and centering the families in a way

00:20:04.620 --> 00:20:08.310
so that first and foremost,
you humanize the issue,

00:20:08.310 --> 00:20:09.600
because we often hear

00:20:09.600 --> 00:20:13.830
so much about the statistics
and it's just numbers.

00:20:13.830 --> 00:20:18.090
And so we really wanted
people to feel as hard

00:20:18.090 --> 00:20:23.090
as it is, to feel what it means to lose

00:20:23.220 --> 00:20:26.997
a mother from childbirth complications.

00:20:26.997 --> 00:20:29.670
So that, that we both knew.

00:20:29.670 --> 00:20:32.340
And at the same time, we
knew it was really important

00:20:32.340 --> 00:20:36.347
to get the information in
about why this was happening.

00:20:36.347 --> 00:20:39.240
We wanted to talk to experts in the field,

00:20:39.240 --> 00:20:40.920
but we didn't want it to be a film

00:20:40.920 --> 00:20:42.300
with just people talking at you.

00:20:42.300 --> 00:20:44.103
We wanted you to go on a journey.

00:20:45.210 --> 00:20:47.913
And so the way we began,

00:20:49.200 --> 00:20:54.200
Shamony Gibson died in October, 2019.

00:20:54.450 --> 00:20:58.366
I wanna say Paula and I met,
must have met November, 2019.

00:20:58.366 --> 00:21:01.680
Shamony died in October.

00:21:01.680 --> 00:21:05.190
In December, her family, her mother,

00:21:05.190 --> 00:21:07.343
put out a call to action.

00:21:07.343 --> 00:21:10.440
They were having an event, a celebration

00:21:10.440 --> 00:21:12.000
of Shamony's life.

00:21:12.000 --> 00:21:15.419
They put it on Instagram
and invited the community.

00:21:15.419 --> 00:21:20.419
Shamony's partner, Omari wanted
to have a convening of men

00:21:20.880 --> 00:21:24.240
to talk about what it means
as a man, as a partner

00:21:24.240 --> 00:21:28.260
to lose someone from
childbirth complications.

00:21:28.260 --> 00:21:29.297
Paula saw this on Instagram

00:21:29.297 --> 00:21:31.983
'cause she's like an Instagram ninja.

00:21:32.910 --> 00:21:33.873
I am not.

00:21:35.120 --> 00:21:39.672
And she called Shawnee and
Shawnee answered her phone

00:21:39.672 --> 00:21:43.182
and said, "Hey, you know,
we'd really like to come

00:21:43.182 --> 00:21:45.960
and film what you guys are doing."

00:21:45.960 --> 00:21:49.530
And miraculously, and
Shawnee speaks to this often,

00:21:49.530 --> 00:21:51.360
like she really goes by

00:21:51.360 --> 00:21:53.310
what she feels in the spirit.

00:21:53.310 --> 00:21:56.010
And Shawnee is an amazing woman herself.

00:21:56.010 --> 00:22:00.090
Someone who has been in the birth justice,

00:22:00.090 --> 00:22:01.680
reproductive justice work.

00:22:01.680 --> 00:22:03.540
As she says in the film, she knew,

00:22:03.540 --> 00:22:05.220
she knows what these issues are.

00:22:05.220 --> 00:22:07.290
She speaks to them often.

00:22:07.290 --> 00:22:10.230
And yet this still
happened to her daughter.

00:22:10.230 --> 00:22:13.080
And so I think being that
social justice warrior

00:22:13.080 --> 00:22:14.850
that she is, she said, okay,

00:22:14.850 --> 00:22:17.580
well I'm gonna stand up
and let the world see.

00:22:17.580 --> 00:22:20.730
Sort of like Bruce, who
is also in the film,

00:22:20.730 --> 00:22:23.700
his partner Amber Rose Isaac passed away.

00:22:23.700 --> 00:22:27.510
Bruce often says that
the reason why he decided

00:22:27.510 --> 00:22:30.390
to participate in the
film is like he thinks

00:22:30.390 --> 00:22:32.493
about Mamie Till.

00:22:32.493 --> 00:22:36.120
Till when Emmett Till was
brutally murdered, Mamie decided

00:22:36.120 --> 00:22:39.210
to open up the casket, have an open casket

00:22:39.210 --> 00:22:42.240
to show his mutilated
body so everyone could see

00:22:42.240 --> 00:22:44.220
what happened to her son.

00:22:44.220 --> 00:22:48.540
And so for Bruce opening up
himself to us as filmmakers,

00:22:48.540 --> 00:22:50.520
to all of us to see his story

00:22:50.520 --> 00:22:52.050
as like opening up the casket

00:22:52.050 --> 00:22:54.150
so we can see what he went through,

00:22:54.150 --> 00:22:55.743
so maybe we learned something.

00:22:57.000 --> 00:23:00.720
And so, you know, Shawnee
was really the beginning.

00:23:00.720 --> 00:23:02.430
Omari says that at first, he was like,

00:23:02.430 --> 00:23:04.950
there's no way he was gonna do this film.

00:23:04.950 --> 00:23:07.590
You know, he was two months into his grief

00:23:07.590 --> 00:23:12.590
and Shawnee too, I mean,
very early in their grief.

00:23:12.824 --> 00:23:15.750
But they let us, they let us come in

00:23:15.750 --> 00:23:18.813
and they let us go through
the process with them.

00:23:19.740 --> 00:23:22.650
After, so Omari had
the first men's circle.

00:23:22.650 --> 00:23:25.100
There were some other
fathers who had lost there.

00:23:26.640 --> 00:23:31.590
So then Amber Rose Isaac
died in April of 2020.

00:23:32.640 --> 00:23:35.280
Omari hears, because this is now,

00:23:35.280 --> 00:23:36.540
they're on a network, right?

00:23:36.540 --> 00:23:37.800
So, so these men now,

00:23:37.800 --> 00:23:40.590
or these families, they hear
of someone else, someone died.

00:23:40.590 --> 00:23:45.590
Omari reaches out to Bruce,
they develop a relationship.

00:23:45.960 --> 00:23:48.180
And because Omari

00:23:48.180 --> 00:23:50.730
and Bruce have this relationship,
Omari explains to Bruce,

00:23:50.730 --> 00:23:52.200
we're making this film.

00:23:52.200 --> 00:23:54.780
And Bruce says, "Okay,
I am gonna go get on."

00:23:54.780 --> 00:23:58.560
But I will tell you before that, Bruce,

00:23:58.560 --> 00:24:02.520
after I wanna say, so
if Amber died in April,

00:24:02.520 --> 00:24:04.680
I wanna say it was maybe even two weeks

00:24:04.680 --> 00:24:07.950
after Amber died, Bruce
had a press conference,

00:24:07.950 --> 00:24:10.260
which we actually filmed
before we knew Bruce,

00:24:10.260 --> 00:24:13.080
because we had heard again on social media

00:24:13.080 --> 00:24:15.420
that he was gonna have
this press conference.

00:24:15.420 --> 00:24:18.270
So we brought out a camera to capture him

00:24:18.270 --> 00:24:20.550
doing his press conference.

00:24:20.550 --> 00:24:22.980
And thereafter we met him

00:24:22.980 --> 00:24:25.950
and continued on the journey with them.

00:24:25.950 --> 00:24:29.320
And I, I'll say, you
know, I make this film

00:24:33.180 --> 00:24:35.883
because like I said before,
it seems obvious to me.

00:24:37.170 --> 00:24:41.820
Infants, mothers, you
know, that is the bedrock,

00:24:41.820 --> 00:24:43.920
the health of women,

00:24:43.920 --> 00:24:46.470
the health of infants is
the health of a nation.

00:24:46.470 --> 00:24:49.170
That's how you mark
the health of a nation.

00:24:49.170 --> 00:24:51.720
And we are not doing well at all.

00:24:51.720 --> 00:24:53.838
And we should be doing better.

00:24:53.838 --> 00:24:58.200
And so I made this film
because I wanna make an impact.

00:24:58.200 --> 00:25:00.270
I mean, when you hear
my bio, like I'm trying

00:25:00.270 --> 00:25:01.890
to make an impact.

00:25:01.890 --> 00:25:05.370
I'm trying to humanize
one, the Black experience,

00:25:05.370 --> 00:25:07.410
because I know as a young Black child

00:25:07.410 --> 00:25:09.270
what it's like to be dehumanized.

00:25:09.270 --> 00:25:12.690
So we wanna humanize our
experience for ourselves

00:25:12.690 --> 00:25:15.437
and for all of us so we
understand who we are

00:25:15.437 --> 00:25:18.540
and so that we can and
we can come together.

00:25:18.540 --> 00:25:20.460
And I also have to say like my partnership

00:25:20.460 --> 00:25:23.010
with Paula in making this
film, I mentioned she

00:25:23.010 --> 00:25:24.990
and I are very different.

00:25:24.990 --> 00:25:27.420
We have very different points of view

00:25:27.420 --> 00:25:29.940
and it can be very difficult

00:25:29.940 --> 00:25:32.940
to have hard conversations about things.

00:25:32.940 --> 00:25:35.580
She doesn't understand my life experience.

00:25:35.580 --> 00:25:37.980
I don't understand her life experience.

00:25:37.980 --> 00:25:39.690
That can create conflict.

00:25:39.690 --> 00:25:42.990
But I think it's really
important that we dig down

00:25:42.990 --> 00:25:44.790
and have those conversations.

00:25:44.790 --> 00:25:46.410
I think when we are made

00:25:46.410 --> 00:25:48.720
to feel most uncomfortable
sometimes the places

00:25:48.720 --> 00:25:50.520
where we grow the most.

00:25:50.520 --> 00:25:55.110
And so as we made this very
difficult, complicated film,

00:25:55.110 --> 00:25:59.130
you know, we also learned
and grew from each other.

00:25:59.130 --> 00:26:03.930
And it's really my hope that
"Aftershock" is able to,

00:26:03.930 --> 00:26:06.360
and that's why it's so amazing

00:26:06.360 --> 00:26:08.453
to be here with you all today.

00:26:08.453 --> 00:26:12.930
Because I think, you
know, as, as people go on

00:26:12.930 --> 00:26:15.210
and their lives and
their careers, I'm hoping

00:26:15.210 --> 00:26:18.570
that "Aftershock" stays in
someone's brain a little bit.

00:26:18.570 --> 00:26:23.280
And especially healthcare
providers, to think about

00:26:23.280 --> 00:26:25.480
how you are treating someone

00:26:27.192 --> 00:26:30.570
and how you can have a real impact.

00:26:30.570 --> 00:26:32.020
We all have an impact I mean,

00:26:32.875 --> 00:26:34.408
and I believe we all have a role to play

00:26:34.408 --> 00:26:36.900
in making this crisis better.

00:26:36.900 --> 00:26:41.900
And for me it's by
storytelling and by standing up

00:26:41.910 --> 00:26:44.040
and by giving voice to
what I can give voice to.

00:26:44.040 --> 00:26:47.109
And I'm humbled by the ability to do that.

00:26:47.109 --> 00:26:50.760
And for you, it may be
something, something different,

00:26:50.760 --> 00:26:52.050
but that's why I am here.

00:26:52.050 --> 00:26:55.080
That's what motivates
me to make "Aftershock"

00:26:55.080 --> 00:26:58.140
and to do the other kinds
of work that I'm working on.

00:26:58.140 --> 00:27:02.190
I will always be advocating
for women and infants' health.

00:27:02.190 --> 00:27:05.190
It is of the most
important thing we can do.

00:27:05.190 --> 00:27:08.010
When I saw the, just this morning

00:27:08.010 --> 00:27:09.570
or yesterday, the fact

00:27:09.570 --> 00:27:12.330
that our infant mortality
rate is going up,

00:27:12.330 --> 00:27:16.920
it's like WTF, how can this be?

00:27:16.920 --> 00:27:20.310
How are we not as a nation putting all

00:27:20.310 --> 00:27:22.500
of our resources to our people?

00:27:22.500 --> 00:27:25.770
I just, I, it just,

00:27:25.770 --> 00:27:29.100
because we are supposed to
be the beacon for the world.

00:27:29.100 --> 00:27:32.490
And yet, and yet we have
these abysmal rates.

00:27:32.490 --> 00:27:35.760
It's, and it's solvable.

00:27:35.760 --> 00:27:38.370
It's not like we can't fix it.

00:27:38.370 --> 00:27:41.190
We can, if we choose to.

00:27:41.190 --> 00:27:45.360
So I will continue to be
fighting and thank you.

00:27:45.360 --> 00:27:47.223
And I look forward to our conversation.

00:27:47.223 --> 00:27:50.473
(audience applauding)

00:28:05.310 --> 00:28:07.710
- See the young leading
the old. (laughing)

00:28:07.710 --> 00:28:09.791
Thank you. (laughing)

00:28:09.791 --> 00:28:12.300
Yeah, thank you so much.

00:28:12.300 --> 00:28:13.800
Those words of wisdom

00:28:13.800 --> 00:28:18.263
and how we can move to
beyond just feeling to doing

00:28:18.263 --> 00:28:20.550
and making an impact.

00:28:20.550 --> 00:28:22.050
It's so important.

00:28:22.050 --> 00:28:24.030
You know, I look at the healthcare system

00:28:24.030 --> 00:28:25.320
in the United States

00:28:25.320 --> 00:28:30.150
and the structural racism
that exists is so pervasive

00:28:30.150 --> 00:28:33.360
and so destructive to particularly

00:28:33.360 --> 00:28:36.990
to marginalized communities
with Black mothers

00:28:36.990 --> 00:28:40.650
and indigenous women having
the worst outcomes in terms

00:28:40.650 --> 00:28:44.825
of not only infant mortality,
but also maternal mortality.

00:28:44.825 --> 00:28:47.340
And I wonder, you know, if we need

00:28:47.340 --> 00:28:51.420
to start looking at the
health profession schools as

00:28:51.420 --> 00:28:53.310
where we need to put our energies,

00:28:53.310 --> 00:28:56.310
because these systems are
so entrenched, you know,

00:28:56.310 --> 00:28:58.927
whether or not they're gonna
change quickly enough to

00:28:58.927 --> 00:29:01.020
make the dramatic changes that we need

00:29:01.020 --> 00:29:03.055
to see in the lives of these families.

00:29:03.055 --> 00:29:05.400
Whether or not we need
to start putting some

00:29:05.400 --> 00:29:07.590
of our energies into transforming

00:29:07.590 --> 00:29:11.340
and educating people in the
health profession schools

00:29:11.340 --> 00:29:16.340
in a way that they become more
appropriate, less, you know,

00:29:17.760 --> 00:29:21.930
influenced by racial, you
know, beliefs or whatever,

00:29:21.930 --> 00:29:26.310
so that we can ultimately
change those structures

00:29:26.310 --> 00:29:28.395
to better accommodate

00:29:28.395 --> 00:29:31.920
and care for women, particularly
those who are marginalized.

00:29:31.920 --> 00:29:33.270
So I guess my question is,

00:29:33.270 --> 00:29:35.490
should we then be putting
a lot more emphasis

00:29:35.490 --> 00:29:38.340
or focus on those health
profession schools,

00:29:38.340 --> 00:29:42.480
not only in creating a
diverse body of, of providers,

00:29:42.480 --> 00:29:45.150
but also educating all of the providers so

00:29:45.150 --> 00:29:48.240
that they can make changes
within the systems?

00:29:48.240 --> 00:29:49.140
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:29:49.140 --> 00:29:52.740
I think, I think first of
all, building the pipeline.

00:29:52.740 --> 00:29:55.480
I think we were, you know,
somehow, I don't know if

00:29:55.480 --> 00:29:58.410
that was you guys, but I
think first of all, I mean,

00:29:58.410 --> 00:29:59.640
and that's down the line,

00:29:59.640 --> 00:30:03.330
but building a pipeline
of healthcare providers,

00:30:03.330 --> 00:30:05.520
looking at young people from,

00:30:05.520 --> 00:30:08.760
I mean like going really young,
like getting them interested

00:30:08.760 --> 00:30:12.750
and excited about the
sciences and healthcare

00:30:12.750 --> 00:30:15.490
and healthcare and community

00:30:16.380 --> 00:30:19.989
and helping them find connectivity ways.

00:30:19.989 --> 00:30:21.840
And I know places like Robert Wood Johnson

00:30:21.840 --> 00:30:23.430
are doing things like that,

00:30:23.430 --> 00:30:24.690
but I think we need more

00:30:24.690 --> 00:30:28.710
and more of that reaching
to reaching out to our Black

00:30:28.710 --> 00:30:32.430
and brown youngsters to get them involved

00:30:32.430 --> 00:30:33.660
in healthcare providing.

00:30:33.660 --> 00:30:36.240
For those that are already there,

00:30:36.240 --> 00:30:39.240
they're absolutely, I
mean it's just amazing.

00:30:39.240 --> 00:30:42.573
Even some of the way
people are taught recently,

00:30:43.890 --> 00:30:47.910
someone showed me a campaign
called Illustrate Change.

00:30:47.910 --> 00:30:50.790
I don't know if you all are
familiar with this, with just

00:30:50.790 --> 00:30:55.080
to show images in textbooks of medical

00:30:55.080 --> 00:30:58.320
textbooks of people who are brown.

00:30:58.320 --> 00:31:00.390
Like it's 2023.

00:31:00.390 --> 00:31:01.770
And you mean to tell me

00:31:01.770 --> 00:31:04.770
that in textbooks we're really
still just seeing mostly just

00:31:04.770 --> 00:31:08.670
white people and things show
up differently on brown skin.

00:31:08.670 --> 00:31:13.410
So just integrating the way,
just naturally things flow

00:31:13.410 --> 00:31:17.694
through so it's not so obscure or odd.

00:31:17.694 --> 00:31:22.240
I also think, it seems
to me that even the way

00:31:23.760 --> 00:31:28.380
the medical profession teaches
it's hard on doctors, right?

00:31:28.380 --> 00:31:30.400
You're dehumanized yourself

00:31:31.290 --> 00:31:33.630
and so you're rushed
through, you gotta hurry up,

00:31:33.630 --> 00:31:36.540
you gotta do, you can't
eat, you gotta grind.

00:31:36.540 --> 00:31:41.540
And I think often that
kind of dehumanization

00:31:41.610 --> 00:31:44.730
if you will, of the healthcare
providers ends up trickling

00:31:44.730 --> 00:31:47.940
down often to the patient as well.

00:31:47.940 --> 00:31:51.120
So is there a more humane way about

00:31:51.120 --> 00:31:52.350
going about the teaching?

00:31:52.350 --> 00:31:55.170
Now I know it's a stressful
job, don't get me wrong,

00:31:55.170 --> 00:31:59.610
but I just think that
there's gotta be a way

00:31:59.610 --> 00:32:01.525
that people, and I,

00:32:01.525 --> 00:32:02.880
I mean this is the hardest
work though, right?

00:32:02.880 --> 00:32:04.593
I mean, how do you teach empathy?

00:32:05.535 --> 00:32:07.200
I mean, that's a hard thing to teach.

00:32:07.200 --> 00:32:09.270
People again, come with life experience

00:32:09.270 --> 00:32:10.710
before they get there.

00:32:10.710 --> 00:32:12.990
You know, we live in a
white supremacist society

00:32:12.990 --> 00:32:15.300
that is driving you in a certain place.

00:32:15.300 --> 00:32:20.300
It's, it has to be active
work to change that mindset.

00:32:21.780 --> 00:32:23.550
So I do think institutions really

00:32:23.550 --> 00:32:24.780
need to be thinking about that.

00:32:24.780 --> 00:32:29.070
And I, I'll end this by
saying, as we all know,

00:32:29.070 --> 00:32:32.880
when you solve the problem
for the most marginalized

00:32:32.880 --> 00:32:36.030
and most vulnerable, you
solve it for everyone.

00:32:36.030 --> 00:32:40.230
And by the way, nobody's
doing all that well compared

00:32:40.230 --> 00:32:42.393
to other industrialized nations.

00:32:46.260 --> 00:32:48.510
- I have a question or
two, but before I ask that,

00:32:48.510 --> 00:32:50.910
I wanna say thank you to you

00:32:50.910 --> 00:32:55.910
for also allowing the school
of nursing and our nursing

00:32:56.130 --> 00:32:58.380
and midwifery students and faculty

00:32:58.380 --> 00:33:02.130
and staff to kind of be
in your glow for a while

00:33:02.130 --> 00:33:05.970
and have you here amongst us.

00:33:05.970 --> 00:33:08.940
So it is very special for us.

00:33:08.940 --> 00:33:13.712
Along the lines of the
educational pipeline,

00:33:13.712 --> 00:33:16.680
three of us here are
actually on the admissions

00:33:16.680 --> 00:33:18.510
committee this year.

00:33:18.510 --> 00:33:21.180
And I would like your opinion

00:33:21.180 --> 00:33:23.280
of the recent Supreme Court decision

00:33:23.280 --> 00:33:26.562
that enforces race blind admissions

00:33:26.562 --> 00:33:31.260
and how you think that
will impact our ability

00:33:31.260 --> 00:33:36.180
to diversify ethnically,
culturally, racially our workforce,

00:33:36.180 --> 00:33:39.030
which has been a priority
of the Yale School

00:33:39.030 --> 00:33:41.010
of Nursing historically.

00:33:41.010 --> 00:33:43.860
And it's very present
for us at this moment

00:33:43.860 --> 00:33:47.310
as we're beginning to
receive applications.

00:33:47.310 --> 00:33:50.010
- Yeah, I can, I can only
imagine what it's like

00:33:50.010 --> 00:33:51.630
working in these institutions with

00:33:51.630 --> 00:33:53.133
that decision coming down.

00:33:56.280 --> 00:33:57.963
First, I feel like,

00:33:59.970 --> 00:34:01.530
the numbers weren't
that high to begin with.

00:34:01.530 --> 00:34:02.670
Let's start with that. Right?

00:34:02.670 --> 00:34:03.690
Let's just be clear.

00:34:03.690 --> 00:34:07.200
I just want to say that
it's not like, you know,

00:34:07.200 --> 00:34:10.320
there were lots of Black and
brown people coming through

00:34:10.320 --> 00:34:13.209
and so, you know, this
decision is stopping the flood

00:34:13.209 --> 00:34:15.780
of unqualified Black and brown people.

00:34:15.780 --> 00:34:18.063
That is just BS. We know that, right?

00:34:19.962 --> 00:34:22.260
So I think, I think it's interesting.

00:34:22.260 --> 00:34:26.432
I wonder how, I know you all
are under a lot of pressure

00:34:26.432 --> 00:34:29.976
and I wonder what the impact will be.

00:34:29.976 --> 00:34:34.430
I have to say I call
out Edward Bloom by name

00:34:34.430 --> 00:34:39.430
because this is a campaign
directed by a person that is not

00:34:39.690 --> 00:34:41.790
who is, who has other people behind him.

00:34:41.790 --> 00:34:43.590
Don't get me wrong, he's not the only one.

00:34:43.590 --> 00:34:47.220
But there is a person who is on a mission

00:34:47.220 --> 00:34:49.140
to take things away from Black

00:34:49.140 --> 00:34:50.940
and brown people, including women.

00:34:50.940 --> 00:34:53.250
And I have to, you mentioned
the affirmative action,

00:34:53.250 --> 00:34:55.740
I'm gonna get back to
school, but he also came

00:34:55.740 --> 00:34:58.740
after the Fearless
Fund, if you don't know,

00:34:58.740 --> 00:35:01.890
which is a venture fund
that supports Black

00:35:01.890 --> 00:35:03.840
and brown women looking

00:35:03.840 --> 00:35:06.360
to have their own economic independence.

00:35:06.360 --> 00:35:10.950
They only give $20,000 per entrepreneur

00:35:10.950 --> 00:35:15.183
and he wants to stop that
when we get like such a tiny

00:35:15.183 --> 00:35:18.930
amount of funding from venture funds.

00:35:18.930 --> 00:35:23.782
So I say that to say
there is an assault coming

00:35:23.782 --> 00:35:27.720
to, and it's amazing because
the numbers are so small.

00:35:27.720 --> 00:35:31.083
So what are you, what
are they so afraid of?

00:35:32.700 --> 00:35:34.920
You know, in some ways I
look and I say, well gosh,

00:35:34.920 --> 00:35:37.980
Black and brown women must
be doing something right.

00:35:37.980 --> 00:35:41.160
You know, we, we must be a
force to be reckoned with.

00:35:41.160 --> 00:35:42.510
And I guess Trump would tell you

00:35:42.510 --> 00:35:44.790
that too when he looks
at who's prosecuting him.

00:35:44.790 --> 00:35:46.923
But, sorry, that's an aside.

00:35:49.363 --> 00:35:50.880
You know, I think you all have a challenge

00:35:50.880 --> 00:35:53.820
and I think, I think your
challenge again goes to

00:35:53.820 --> 00:35:56.190
what happens before
the admissions process.

00:35:56.190 --> 00:35:59.100
And I don't know what
you can do about that,

00:35:59.100 --> 00:36:02.700
but I feel like there
needs again to, you need

00:36:02.700 --> 00:36:06.600
to start way earlier,
getting young people ready

00:36:06.600 --> 00:36:09.420
to get here, get excited, and be here

00:36:09.420 --> 00:36:13.026
and be able to be
admitted in the right way.

00:36:13.026 --> 00:36:16.620
And I think that's what's
gonna have to change.

00:36:16.620 --> 00:36:19.320
I also think you're gonna
have to be crafty, you know?

00:36:20.692 --> 00:36:22.710
You know, I don't, I can't remember

00:36:22.710 --> 00:36:24.330
what it's like applying to grad school.

00:36:24.330 --> 00:36:26.698
It was so long ago that I did.

00:36:26.698 --> 00:36:31.580
But really like looking
at who the whole person is

00:36:32.760 --> 00:36:35.010
and understanding who that person is

00:36:35.010 --> 00:36:38.362
and really making sure they're
supported once they get here.

00:36:38.362 --> 00:36:40.440
I mean, I think that's the other thing.

00:36:40.440 --> 00:36:42.420
I mean, you know, it's
one thing to be admitted,

00:36:42.420 --> 00:36:44.370
it's another thing to make it through.

00:36:44.370 --> 00:36:49.370
And people really need a lot
of support when they are here.

00:36:49.380 --> 00:36:54.238
So I think you're up against
a lot of challenges right now.

00:36:54.238 --> 00:36:58.380
It's a tough time and for
those of you who are here,

00:36:58.380 --> 00:37:00.445
I mean it, you're in a battle.

00:37:00.445 --> 00:37:02.490
I mean, you're on the
front, I mean this is

00:37:02.490 --> 00:37:05.070
frontline battle stuff.

00:37:05.070 --> 00:37:07.920
Put your armor on 'cause
they're gonna keep coming.

00:37:07.920 --> 00:37:09.600
They might, you know, you got here,

00:37:09.600 --> 00:37:10.590
you're getting through it.

00:37:10.590 --> 00:37:12.180
I'm sure there, there are complications

00:37:12.180 --> 00:37:13.830
and difficulties while you're here

00:37:13.830 --> 00:37:15.390
and they're gonna be when you get outta

00:37:15.390 --> 00:37:17.610
here, wherever you go after that.

00:37:17.610 --> 00:37:20.340
So put your armor on,
get your battle gear,

00:37:20.340 --> 00:37:22.680
make sure you've got the people
around you that support you

00:37:22.680 --> 00:37:24.840
and understand what you're doing

00:37:24.840 --> 00:37:27.030
because it doesn't get easier.

00:37:27.030 --> 00:37:30.240
And don't give up because
you're doing something right,

00:37:30.240 --> 00:37:33.363
because that's why
they're trying to stop us.

00:37:34.740 --> 00:37:36.870
- Would it be okay if
I just ask our nursing

00:37:36.870 --> 00:37:39.320
and midwifery students to
stand up that are here?

00:37:40.650 --> 00:37:43.321
Would you guys be up to, be up to that?

00:37:43.321 --> 00:37:46.488
(audience applauding)

00:37:48.420 --> 00:37:49.720
- I'm excited to see them.

00:37:50.601 --> 00:37:52.560
I just, I just wanna really thank you

00:37:52.560 --> 00:37:53.850
for doing what you're doing.

00:37:53.850 --> 00:37:56.010
It's really important work.

00:37:56.010 --> 00:37:58.310
So, so thank you and
thank you for being here.

00:38:00.480 --> 00:38:04.320
- My second question is,
if you can talk about this,

00:38:04.320 --> 00:38:08.040
what's your next equity related project

00:38:08.040 --> 00:38:10.529
that you're thinking about working on?

00:38:10.529 --> 00:38:11.813
Where are you going from here?

00:38:13.050 --> 00:38:15.450
- Very different kind of project.

00:38:15.450 --> 00:38:17.310
I've done some heavy stuff.

00:38:17.310 --> 00:38:20.340
I did, as you heard, I,
I made the film "Monster"

00:38:20.340 --> 00:38:23.495
before this, which is about
a 17 year-old kid who's

00:38:23.495 --> 00:38:26.610
incarcerated for his part in a robbery

00:38:26.610 --> 00:38:28.740
and then did "Aftershock".

00:38:28.740 --> 00:38:31.865
So what I'm working on right now is,

00:38:31.865 --> 00:38:33.300
and I don't know if it'll
actually see the light

00:38:33.300 --> 00:38:34.980
of day, but I'm working on it.

00:38:34.980 --> 00:38:39.303
It's a script that is a feature film,

00:38:40.410 --> 00:38:44.910
that's horror, that deals a little bit

00:38:44.910 --> 00:38:48.660
with, well I'll just
tell you, it's, you know,

00:38:48.660 --> 00:38:50.881
the land in Martha's Vineyard.

00:38:50.881 --> 00:38:51.716
I think a lot of people think

00:38:51.716 --> 00:38:55.260
of Martha's Vineyard
these days as one thing.

00:38:55.260 --> 00:38:59.460
What it is, it's funny, if
you Google Martha's Vineyard,

00:38:59.460 --> 00:39:03.540
it begins, the story of
Martha's Vineyard begins

00:39:03.540 --> 00:39:06.390
with white people
showing up on the shores.

00:39:06.390 --> 00:39:09.570
Like there were not
indigenous people there

00:39:09.570 --> 00:39:12.120
who have been there since 20,000 BC.

00:39:12.120 --> 00:39:16.860
So I'm having a little
bit of fun trying to,

00:39:16.860 --> 00:39:18.360
and I, you know, of course I'm making it

00:39:18.360 --> 00:39:19.620
so complicated for myself.

00:39:19.620 --> 00:39:22.050
My son is like, I don't know
why you're working so hard.

00:39:22.050 --> 00:39:23.400
Look at all that crap on Netflix.

00:39:23.400 --> 00:39:24.810
I don't know why, you know,

00:39:24.810 --> 00:39:29.160
but I wanna say something about, you know,

00:39:29.160 --> 00:39:32.190
the land and the trauma

00:39:32.190 --> 00:39:37.190
and the fact that what happened

00:39:37.200 --> 00:39:40.560
before we're still
dealing with today in ways

00:39:40.560 --> 00:39:42.240
that we don't even understand.

00:39:42.240 --> 00:39:46.470
And you know, again, just, you know,

00:39:46.470 --> 00:39:49.890
trying to write something that's kind of

00:39:49.890 --> 00:39:52.920
surprising in the fact
that I'm actually dealing

00:39:52.920 --> 00:39:55.023
with something that's really important.

00:40:12.660 --> 00:40:16.033
- So we talked a little
bit about this in the car

00:40:17.130 --> 00:40:22.130
that there's a framework
of quality maternal-

00:40:36.437 --> 00:40:38.790
The battery doesn't work.

00:40:38.790 --> 00:40:40.293
Can you hear me now? Oh yeah.

00:40:42.180 --> 00:40:44.460
There's a framework on quality maternal

00:40:44.460 --> 00:40:48.120
and newborn care that's
based on evidence that was

00:40:48.120 --> 00:40:50.043
produced 10 years ago on,

00:40:52.560 --> 00:40:54.330
particularly around midwifery.

00:40:54.330 --> 00:40:58.260
And it, and it has five
components that look at

00:40:58.260 --> 00:41:01.680
how we practice, who practices,

00:41:01.680 --> 00:41:06.630
who the workers are, how the
practices based in values

00:41:06.630 --> 00:41:10.770
and philosophy and how it's organized.

00:41:10.770 --> 00:41:14.940
Can women get to it? Can they,
is it acceptable to them?

00:41:14.940 --> 00:41:19.940
So those pretty well make up
all the things that all women

00:41:19.950 --> 00:41:21.570
and newborns should have.

00:41:21.570 --> 00:41:26.250
So if you had one area to invest in

00:41:26.250 --> 00:41:28.923
that you think would make the most impact,

00:41:29.970 --> 00:41:31.413
what would you invest in?

00:41:35.400 --> 00:41:37.350
- I am laughing because
she asked me this question

00:41:37.350 --> 00:41:38.970
earlier, not quite like that.

00:41:38.970 --> 00:41:40.380
And I said in my, she was like,

00:41:40.380 --> 00:41:42.630
if there's one thing I could
have, what would it be?

00:41:42.630 --> 00:41:44.463
And I said, Jeff Bezos' money.

00:41:46.890 --> 00:41:49.718
And so now, now she's like, okay,

00:41:49.718 --> 00:41:52.050
so if you had Jeff Bezos money,
what would be the one thing

00:41:52.050 --> 00:41:53.673
that you would invest in?

00:41:55.920 --> 00:42:00.000
You know, I, the reason
why it's hard for me

00:42:00.000 --> 00:42:02.190
to answer it like that is

00:42:02.190 --> 00:42:05.850
because this is a multi-pronged problem.

00:42:05.850 --> 00:42:08.610
And so you need a multi-pronged approach.

00:42:08.610 --> 00:42:11.250
That is, to me, the crux of the problem.

00:42:11.250 --> 00:42:13.590
Everyone keeps trying to go to one thing.

00:42:13.590 --> 00:42:15.540
Like if I just go to the community

00:42:15.540 --> 00:42:18.960
and I do this, then the
problem will be solved.

00:42:18.960 --> 00:42:22.230
And that's why I resist
answering it like that.

00:42:22.230 --> 00:42:26.220
And I say I need a lot of money so

00:42:26.220 --> 00:42:30.480
that I can put money towards politics so

00:42:30.480 --> 00:42:32.220
that we can affect policy,

00:42:32.220 --> 00:42:36.630
we can elect good people into
office that who are going to

00:42:36.630 --> 00:42:40.590
put forth the kind of
policies like making sure

00:42:40.590 --> 00:42:44.883
that all women get postpartum
care that is covered,

00:42:46.140 --> 00:42:50.100
that we can make sure that all
women can get midwifery care.

00:42:50.100 --> 00:42:52.440
What I would do is I would, I would try

00:42:52.440 --> 00:42:55.830
to help the midwives
get their act together.

00:42:55.830 --> 00:42:56.670
I love midwives.

00:42:56.670 --> 00:42:58.620
I don't mean that in a a pejorative,

00:42:58.620 --> 00:43:00.360
I think midwives are amazing,

00:43:00.360 --> 00:43:04.050
but what I mean by that is
really help build, you know,

00:43:04.050 --> 00:43:07.579
a truly robust midwifery

00:43:07.579 --> 00:43:10.230
program in the United States.

00:43:10.230 --> 00:43:13.920
I mean, the fact that
we're the only country,

00:43:13.920 --> 00:43:15.480
the only industrialized country

00:43:15.480 --> 00:43:18.541
that does not have midwifery
care integrated into our

00:43:18.541 --> 00:43:21.990
healthcare system is insane to me.

00:43:21.990 --> 00:43:25.860
And so I would really try to
figure out how to do that.

00:43:25.860 --> 00:43:29.100
How to, I mean, I would,
I would invest in,

00:43:29.100 --> 00:43:32.580
at HBCU getting midwifery programs started

00:43:32.580 --> 00:43:35.340
and going, going even younger
talk, you know, programs

00:43:35.340 --> 00:43:38.463
that get young people thinking
about midwifery programs,

00:43:39.360 --> 00:43:42.810
investing in more birthing
centers, again, state by state.

00:43:42.810 --> 00:43:45.610
So I gotta deal with the
politics to get that happening.

00:43:47.460 --> 00:43:50.773
But I do think that midwifery
care is a huge answer

00:43:50.773 --> 00:43:55.500
and I think that helping to fund them

00:43:55.500 --> 00:43:57.420
and bring them together and set them up

00:43:57.420 --> 00:44:02.403
for success would be, would be
a huge, huge in this country.

00:44:10.290 --> 00:44:13.050
- Sorry, I have my words here
'cause I'm a little nervous.

00:44:13.050 --> 00:44:17.760
No, this is, thank you for
being here and letting us, yeah.

00:44:17.760 --> 00:44:19.593
Just be with you here.

00:44:21.000 --> 00:44:23.580
If you feel comfortable
speaking personally,

00:44:23.580 --> 00:44:27.000
what was the impact of
making this film on you?

00:44:27.000 --> 00:44:30.573
And as you hold all that
was in that film, I just,

00:44:32.880 --> 00:44:35.880
it's like what and what,
how do you find the strength

00:44:35.880 --> 00:44:38.460
to kind of keep moving
forward with this work?

00:44:38.460 --> 00:44:42.123
I mean, you've been doing it a
while and to just keep going?

00:44:43.950 --> 00:44:48.600
- Yeah, I have to say that Shawnee, Omari

00:44:48.600 --> 00:44:53.205
and Bruce are amazing human beings who

00:44:53.205 --> 00:44:57.660
lost and picked up.

00:44:57.660 --> 00:45:00.570
And because I look at,

00:45:00.570 --> 00:45:04.980
I look at them obviously never
having met Shamony or Amber,

00:45:04.980 --> 00:45:09.090
and I can see what loving,

00:45:09.090 --> 00:45:14.090
loved on beautiful
spirits these women were.

00:45:14.610 --> 00:45:16.020
And I see that

00:45:16.020 --> 00:45:19.140
because their families hold them up

00:45:19.140 --> 00:45:20.790
on the work that they do.

00:45:20.790 --> 00:45:25.790
And you know, I say that, you
know, if Shawnee can get up

00:45:26.730 --> 00:45:30.270
and do the work, I
certainly can't be laying on

00:45:30.270 --> 00:45:31.590
the floor crying about it.

00:45:31.590 --> 00:45:34.540
I need to get up and help support her

00:45:35.747 --> 00:45:38.760
and any way that I, any way that I can.

00:45:38.760 --> 00:45:43.697
And so the impact on
me, I mean, it is hard.

00:45:43.697 --> 00:45:44.880
I, you know, I hadn't seen,

00:45:44.880 --> 00:45:47.040
I just saw the film again last week

00:45:47.040 --> 00:45:49.050
after having not seen it for a while.

00:45:49.050 --> 00:45:54.050
And you know, it's hard.

00:45:55.080 --> 00:45:57.660
But then I look at Omari
and I look at Shawnee

00:45:57.660 --> 00:46:00.000
and I look at Bruce
today, I mean, we met them

00:46:00.000 --> 00:46:01.410
so early in their grief.

00:46:01.410 --> 00:46:06.360
First of all, they look different,
right? Grief is physical.

00:46:06.360 --> 00:46:10.080
And so they look different.

00:46:10.080 --> 00:46:15.080
They're doing amazing work.
The children are thriving.

00:46:15.820 --> 00:46:20.610
And so honestly, I feel
somewhat empowered.

00:46:20.610 --> 00:46:23.583
There were times though, I,
no, no, I mean, it was hard.

00:46:24.720 --> 00:46:26.306
I mean, you know, there are
other people that we talked to

00:46:26.306 --> 00:46:27.630
and you know, I try to be so professional.

00:46:27.630 --> 00:46:30.420
I will say like, you, you
really do steel yourself, right?

00:46:30.420 --> 00:46:32.040
Like, I'm gonna be a professional.

00:46:32.040 --> 00:46:34.380
I'm not gonna show emotion.

00:46:34.380 --> 00:46:37.290
But I remember talking to
some mother, another father

00:46:37.290 --> 00:46:39.930
and him explaining the death of his wife

00:46:39.930 --> 00:46:44.040
and just, I felt so bad
because I just broke.

00:46:44.040 --> 00:46:47.040
I broke down and he's there
saying to me, "It's okay".

00:46:47.040 --> 00:46:49.980
And I'm like, no, it's not
your job to comfort me.

00:46:49.980 --> 00:46:52.563
It's my job to comfort you. You know?

00:46:53.520 --> 00:46:57.324
But, and also, you know,

00:46:57.324 --> 00:47:00.300
when I look at people like Helena Grant,

00:47:00.300 --> 00:47:02.760
who's a midwife in the
film, who gave us a lot

00:47:02.760 --> 00:47:06.360
of information and Neel Shah

00:47:06.360 --> 00:47:09.714
and I look at the history
that, that we've been through,

00:47:09.714 --> 00:47:12.450
I will say I'm just grateful, honestly.

00:47:12.450 --> 00:47:15.780
I'm just really grateful and
I know it was a blessing.

00:47:15.780 --> 00:47:16.890
I know this was a blessing.

00:47:16.890 --> 00:47:17.850
I was where I was supposed

00:47:17.850 --> 00:47:22.053
to be in the right place at
the right time to do the work.

00:47:23.430 --> 00:47:25.170
And I'm sit back now

00:47:25.170 --> 00:47:26.970
and I'm like, oh my God, I
can't believe we did that.

00:47:26.970 --> 00:47:30.900
It feels so easy now that you're
out of it. But it was hard.

00:47:30.900 --> 00:47:34.800
It was really, really
hard in a lot of ways.

00:47:34.800 --> 00:47:38.910
And doing it, I worked out,
I kept myself working out,

00:47:38.910 --> 00:47:41.490
I kept myself eating really well.

00:47:41.490 --> 00:47:44.813
I kept boundaries with the team so

00:47:44.813 --> 00:47:47.130
that we could do the work

00:47:47.130 --> 00:47:49.470
and not get completely overtaken by it.

00:47:49.470 --> 00:47:52.953
Because it is, it is tough, hard stuff.

00:47:58.140 --> 00:48:01.390
(audience applauding)

00:48:23.319 --> 00:48:24.953
- (speaking faintly) Students
at Yale School of Nursing.

00:48:26.520 --> 00:48:29.580
My question is, with the
aftermath of the film,

00:48:29.580 --> 00:48:31.260
since it did take place in New York City,

00:48:31.260 --> 00:48:34.770
which has such a high
disparity between Black

00:48:34.770 --> 00:48:38.760
and white mothers or birth
persons, did any politicians

00:48:38.760 --> 00:48:42.480
or any community workers,

00:48:42.480 --> 00:48:45.540
did anyone reach out to you
about legislative change

00:48:45.540 --> 00:48:46.950
after watching the film?

00:48:46.950 --> 00:48:48.510
- I really appreciate that question.

00:48:48.510 --> 00:48:53.510
In fact, yesterday I just
had a debriefing with

00:48:53.580 --> 00:48:57.971
a few deputy mayors, the
Department of Health,

00:48:57.971 --> 00:49:00.180
a lot of people in New York.

00:49:00.180 --> 00:49:03.930
And a couple of months ago I met with two

00:49:03.930 --> 00:49:06.600
of the deputy mayors,
well the deputy mayor

00:49:06.600 --> 00:49:10.350
and another deputy mayor,
two Black women who sat down

00:49:10.350 --> 00:49:13.800
with me and said, "We
want our legacy to be

00:49:13.800 --> 00:49:15.240
that we've improved the outcomes

00:49:15.240 --> 00:49:18.420
or at least put things in
place to improve outcomes."

00:49:18.420 --> 00:49:22.470
So it is coming, I think
this morning I think,

00:49:22.470 --> 00:49:26.775
or last night there was,

00:49:26.775 --> 00:49:30.030
oops, there was just
announced like a healthy,

00:49:30.030 --> 00:49:30.990
healthy New York.

00:49:30.990 --> 00:49:35.990
And within that there is a
cutout from maternal health.

00:49:36.450 --> 00:49:39.870
And so yes, they have reached out.

00:49:39.870 --> 00:49:42.120
They do know how bad it is.

00:49:42.120 --> 00:49:47.120
And I think especially these
Black women deputy mayors

00:49:47.895 --> 00:49:50.250
want to leave office

00:49:50.250 --> 00:49:54.330
whenever that may be, knowing
they've done what they can

00:49:54.330 --> 00:49:56.523
for Black and brown
women in New York City.

00:50:02.340 --> 00:50:03.600
- Hi, good afternoon panel.

00:50:03.600 --> 00:50:06.270
I'm also a student in the DNP program

00:50:06.270 --> 00:50:07.860
and of course I've shared with you

00:50:07.860 --> 00:50:10.800
before that I've shown your
film a number of times.

00:50:10.800 --> 00:50:13.710
I think what's important
that I wanted to piggyback on

00:50:13.710 --> 00:50:17.550
that you said earlier was the
fact that the issues as far

00:50:17.550 --> 00:50:19.290
as things being preventable,

00:50:19.290 --> 00:50:22.170
the maternal health issues
being preventable is like right

00:50:22.170 --> 00:50:24.720
here in Connecticut too,
as the program director

00:50:24.720 --> 00:50:26.130
of maternal health equity,

00:50:26.130 --> 00:50:29.010
there's a maternal mortality
review committee right here in

00:50:29.010 --> 00:50:31.740
Connecticut that says 80% of the deaths

00:50:31.740 --> 00:50:33.930
that occur could have been prevented.

00:50:33.930 --> 00:50:36.420
So I just wanted to bring it on home so

00:50:36.420 --> 00:50:39.360
that it's not a matter of
looking from just a national lens

00:50:39.360 --> 00:50:42.150
of where we are with these
statistics and these numbers,

00:50:42.150 --> 00:50:45.100
but knowing that this is hitting
really close to home in a,

00:50:46.018 --> 00:50:48.000
really, in a way that we can improve on.

00:50:48.000 --> 00:50:50.848
- Thank you for that. Thank
you. And and you're right.

00:50:50.848 --> 00:50:52.050
I mean, 80% are preventable.

00:50:52.050 --> 00:50:54.060
And then, you know,
the other thing that I,

00:50:54.060 --> 00:50:55.650
that I often don't talk about when I just

00:50:55.650 --> 00:50:57.219
mention is the near deaths.

00:50:57.219 --> 00:51:00.090
You know, how many women nearly die

00:51:00.090 --> 00:51:01.320
from childbirth complications?

00:51:01.320 --> 00:51:03.220
That's not even caught in that number.

00:51:10.680 --> 00:51:12.240
- Hello, my name is Jenny Rebecca.

00:51:12.240 --> 00:51:15.423
I'm a nurse midwifery
student here. Brooklynite.

00:51:17.378 --> 00:51:18.353
- -[Tonya] Brooklyn's in The House.

00:51:20.597 --> 00:51:23.490
- Before I came here, I was
a doula for about six years.

00:51:23.490 --> 00:51:25.986
And the first birth I went to, I was,

00:51:25.986 --> 00:51:27.287
I realized I wanted to be a midwife.

00:51:27.287 --> 00:51:29.190
So I went back to school, did nursing.

00:51:29.190 --> 00:51:30.570
And it's interesting because this film,

00:51:30.570 --> 00:51:31.530
it was hard for me to watch.

00:51:31.530 --> 00:51:33.330
It took four or five goes

00:51:33.330 --> 00:51:35.757
because these were the
people I worked with.

00:51:35.757 --> 00:51:37.577
The Black women in New York

00:51:37.577 --> 00:51:39.540
were the ones that would come to me

00:51:39.540 --> 00:51:41.520
and the doula was the answer.

00:51:41.520 --> 00:51:46.170
And then as like time went
on, the doula was the bandaid,

00:51:46.170 --> 00:51:48.930
and then the midwife was the
bandaid for this problem.

00:51:48.930 --> 00:51:51.780
And working with the Black
woman I did who, you know,

00:51:51.780 --> 00:51:53.310
had doctorates from Harvard

00:51:53.310 --> 00:51:56.280
and, you know, had three like degrees

00:51:56.280 --> 00:51:58.440
and were making like this much money.

00:51:58.440 --> 00:52:00.450
And they would always tell me like, am I,

00:52:00.450 --> 00:52:03.120
like they would say that to
their provider in front of me.

00:52:03.120 --> 00:52:05.850
And it's like, that's, that's
not enough. It doesn't matter.

00:52:05.850 --> 00:52:07.950
And to have been on that side as a doula

00:52:07.950 --> 00:52:10.800
and an advocate was hard

00:52:10.800 --> 00:52:14.070
because you watch just like
all those sly little things

00:52:14.070 --> 00:52:15.496
that do happen and how people do like

00:52:15.496 --> 00:52:17.359
fall through the cracks.

00:52:17.359 --> 00:52:20.250
And then becoming a
nurse, I was more aware

00:52:20.250 --> 00:52:21.810
and I understood how the systems work.

00:52:21.810 --> 00:52:24.750
And now being here, having
the education that I do, like,

00:52:24.750 --> 00:52:28.770
I'm well aware that we are
oppressed when we are on

00:52:28.770 --> 00:52:30.150
labor and birth floors.

00:52:30.150 --> 00:52:31.560
And I don't wanna be one

00:52:31.560 --> 00:52:33.720
of the providers that doesn't listen.

00:52:33.720 --> 00:52:36.060
And I truly do believe that we have

00:52:36.060 --> 00:52:39.270
to like dismantle these systems
like the way hospitals work,

00:52:39.270 --> 00:52:43.110
the way healthcare like, like
insurance is like the bane

00:52:43.110 --> 00:52:45.660
of all of like, how we
have these problems.

00:52:45.660 --> 00:52:47.984
Like Medicare gets cut off,
like Medicaid gets cut off

00:52:47.984 --> 00:52:50.580
as soon as post for postpartum.

00:52:50.580 --> 00:52:53.880
And it's like, one of the
reasons I came here is

00:52:53.880 --> 00:52:56.369
because I knew that we
have to change education

00:52:56.369 --> 00:52:58.050
and like, I think before
my last breath, I want

00:52:58.050 --> 00:53:02.070
to like change the way we're
taught about maternal mortality

00:53:02.070 --> 00:53:05.425
and how we're taught about
obstetric care in textbooks.

00:53:05.425 --> 00:53:10.425
And I guess like when I look
at films like this, you know,

00:53:11.340 --> 00:53:15.900
I want the people that are
making decisions for care

00:53:15.900 --> 00:53:19.320
and do these like, you know, ethic

00:53:19.320 --> 00:53:21.480
and bias workshops in their hospitals

00:53:21.480 --> 00:53:24.450
to actually really understand
like it's the smallest little

00:53:24.450 --> 00:53:27.690
moment to stop and not have
35 patients on your roster

00:53:27.690 --> 00:53:30.660
and say, can I stop for
this one person to listen

00:53:30.660 --> 00:53:33.420
that shortness of breath
or the pain in their chest

00:53:33.420 --> 00:53:35.730
or the pain in their arm
that doesn't quite line up

00:53:35.730 --> 00:53:38.730
with preeclampsia and
maybe that's what it is.

00:53:38.730 --> 00:53:42.420
And to like not worry about
what things cost to send someone

00:53:42.420 --> 00:53:43.740
in for a workup.

00:53:43.740 --> 00:53:45.300
And it's like, it's frustrating

00:53:45.300 --> 00:53:48.990
'cause now I see every
single side of this problem

00:53:48.990 --> 00:53:52.140
and then the weathering
and then systemic racism

00:53:52.140 --> 00:53:54.733
and this going back over 200 years

00:53:54.733 --> 00:53:58.380
and yeah, I, maybe it's just an echo

00:53:58.380 --> 00:54:00.573
of what everyone's has said so far,

00:54:01.421 --> 00:54:02.254
but being on the side,

00:54:02.254 --> 00:54:03.900
it's like you really
realize the importance

00:54:03.900 --> 00:54:06.750
and it's the word that you
used to steel yourself.

00:54:06.750 --> 00:54:08.670
Like that's the only way to like,

00:54:08.670 --> 00:54:11.070
but I'm getting through
this to figure out like,

00:54:11.070 --> 00:54:15.900
how can I be effective without
being on the floor crying.

00:54:15.900 --> 00:54:17.670
- Yeah, thank you for that.

00:54:17.670 --> 00:54:19.560
You know, as I listen to
you talk, I think about it,

00:54:19.560 --> 00:54:22.710
I know you, I know you
all know Jenny Joseph,

00:54:22.710 --> 00:54:26.460
I've had the opportunity to
be with her and hear her speak

00:54:26.460 --> 00:54:28.170
and one of the things that, you know, I

00:54:28.170 --> 00:54:31.530
so appreciate about her is
she's so blunt and direct

00:54:31.530 --> 00:54:35.490
and she's like, I'm tired of
talking about the problem.

00:54:35.490 --> 00:54:40.413
Now I need to build a system
outside of the system.

00:54:40.413 --> 00:54:42.270
Like, like that is what she's thinking.

00:54:42.270 --> 00:54:45.300
And like, it's like how
do we do that? Like that?

00:54:45.300 --> 00:54:46.950
That's the thing, right?

00:54:46.950 --> 00:54:49.500
But again, you know, you,
you think about that, right?

00:54:49.500 --> 00:54:51.780
Okay, we're gonna build
it outside of the system

00:54:51.780 --> 00:54:54.330
so women don't have to deal.

00:54:54.330 --> 00:54:57.690
But then in places like Alabama

00:54:57.690 --> 00:55:01.380
where the outcomes in
hospitals were terrible

00:55:01.380 --> 00:55:04.350
and the midwives and the
community said, you know what?

00:55:04.350 --> 00:55:05.940
We're gonna build
outside of the community.

00:55:05.940 --> 00:55:07.740
We're gonna build outside of the system.

00:55:07.740 --> 00:55:10.350
We're gonna set up our
own midwifery practice.

00:55:10.350 --> 00:55:12.210
Black women are like, great,
we're gonna come there.

00:55:12.210 --> 00:55:13.980
And then the state comes
and gets in their business

00:55:13.980 --> 00:55:15.660
and says, oh no, you can't do that.

00:55:15.660 --> 00:55:17.070
We want you all to go to hospitals,

00:55:17.070 --> 00:55:19.200
but our outcomes are better.

00:55:19.200 --> 00:55:22.246
So you say you wanna protect women,

00:55:22.246 --> 00:55:24.660
but you're gonna send us
back into the hospital

00:55:24.660 --> 00:55:26.520
where the outcomes are worse.

00:55:26.520 --> 00:55:29.670
So what are you really,
oh, you're policing

00:55:29.670 --> 00:55:31.440
women is what, what you're doing.

00:55:31.440 --> 00:55:33.030
You don't want us to figure it out.

00:55:33.030 --> 00:55:36.030
So it's, I mean, it is, it's hard.

00:55:36.030 --> 00:55:41.030
It's really hard and really
frustrating, which is again,

00:55:41.130 --> 00:55:44.490
why I need Jeff Bezos' money so I can help

00:55:44.490 --> 00:55:46.590
get better politicians in Alabama

00:55:46.590 --> 00:55:48.090
so I can support those women

00:55:48.090 --> 00:55:50.313
who are setting up their birthing centers.

00:55:57.390 --> 00:55:58.800
- Good evening. My name is Tamika.

00:55:58.800 --> 00:56:03.060
I'm part of the YSN faculty
here at the School of Nursing.

00:56:03.060 --> 00:56:05.730
And I had, bear with me, I'm gonna try

00:56:05.730 --> 00:56:07.713
to do this without getting emotional.

00:56:08.700 --> 00:56:12.510
I had my first maternal
experience three months ago when I

00:56:12.510 --> 00:56:16.695
gave birth to, at 24
weeks to a micro preemie.

00:56:16.695 --> 00:56:18.270
My daughter, her name is Marissa,

00:56:18.270 --> 00:56:21.120
she's still at Yale New Haven Hospital,

00:56:21.120 --> 00:56:23.520
neonatal intensive care unit.

00:56:23.520 --> 00:56:26.280
And when I think about

00:56:26.280 --> 00:56:30.240
how infant mortality is
deeply rooted in systemic

00:56:30.240 --> 00:56:32.607
and structural racism, I know

00:56:32.607 --> 00:56:35.940
and I understand that I have a Black baby

00:56:35.940 --> 00:56:37.830
that's being taken care of by

00:56:37.830 --> 00:56:41.490
a predominantly white healthcare team.

00:56:41.490 --> 00:56:44.147
And I have lost sleep over that.

00:56:44.147 --> 00:56:48.150
'Cause she cannot talk, she
cannot advocate for herself.

00:56:48.150 --> 00:56:51.480
And it's something that I
worry about every single day.

00:56:51.480 --> 00:56:53.970
Now, don't get me wrong,
the team has been amazing.

00:56:53.970 --> 00:56:57.270
All the nurses and doctors I, you know,

00:56:57.270 --> 00:56:59.510
had a good experience so far.

00:56:59.510 --> 00:57:03.220
But it's something that
it, it's statistics

00:57:04.146 --> 00:57:05.046
and it's the facts

00:57:05.959 --> 00:57:07.560
that I'm there every day at her bedside,

00:57:07.560 --> 00:57:09.480
trying to have a voice and advocate.

00:57:09.480 --> 00:57:11.688
I'm there. They know me by my first name.

00:57:11.688 --> 00:57:14.280
I'm always there when they're
rounding on her talking

00:57:14.280 --> 00:57:15.813
about her medical care.

00:57:16.950 --> 00:57:18.990
But it's something that's like,

00:57:18.990 --> 00:57:23.130
it's my life right now being
her voice, being there,

00:57:23.130 --> 00:57:25.503
speaking up, asking questions,

00:57:26.370 --> 00:57:29.820
and you know, giving birth
is just one part of it.

00:57:29.820 --> 00:57:34.410
It now translates to
making sure she, you know,

00:57:34.410 --> 00:57:37.230
she has complications of
course being born at 24 weeks.

00:57:37.230 --> 00:57:39.540
But, you know, just being there

00:57:39.540 --> 00:57:42.090
and making sure like are
we doing the right things

00:57:42.090 --> 00:57:44.340
and just being involved in her care.

00:57:44.340 --> 00:57:48.330
So, you know, I think it, yes,
the diversity is so important

00:57:48.330 --> 00:57:53.330
that we are starting at
diversifying the healthcare force

00:57:53.460 --> 00:57:55.980
and having more diverse midwives

00:57:55.980 --> 00:57:58.410
and starting with admissions committee

00:57:58.410 --> 00:58:03.410
and, you know, enrolling
more healthcare workers of,

00:58:03.570 --> 00:58:05.280
you know, more diverse backgrounds

00:58:05.280 --> 00:58:09.000
and how important that is
to see, you know, nurses

00:58:09.000 --> 00:58:12.780
and doctors who are of
color that can relate

00:58:12.780 --> 00:58:14.406
to our lived experiences.

00:58:14.406 --> 00:58:16.690
And it's just so important

00:58:18.021 --> 00:58:19.291
because we are taking care of patients

00:58:19.291 --> 00:58:20.479
and this is the community

00:58:20.479 --> 00:58:23.790
and this is the population
that deserve to have, you know,

00:58:23.790 --> 00:58:25.443
people that look like us.

00:58:27.960 --> 00:58:30.003
- God bless you, God bless Marissa.

00:58:32.070 --> 00:58:33.483
And how are you doing?

00:58:34.436 --> 00:58:36.180
- Good.
- You're doing okay?

00:58:36.180 --> 00:58:38.820
Because I know, I know
we're worried about Marissa,

00:58:38.820 --> 00:58:42.003
but you know, you gotta
be okay too, right?

00:58:42.990 --> 00:58:46.150
And I understand how you feel.

00:58:50.070 --> 00:58:52.860
That's a scary thing leaving your child

00:58:52.860 --> 00:58:55.140
with strangers who don't look like you.

00:58:55.140 --> 00:58:57.630
And yet here you are, you're
a, you're a nursing student,

00:58:57.630 --> 00:59:01.650
so, so you know the things
to say, the language to use

00:59:01.650 --> 00:59:04.170
to let them know that, you know, right?

00:59:04.170 --> 00:59:07.740
Imagine, and that's a
blessing and it's still scary.

00:59:07.740 --> 00:59:09.510
Imagine you didn't have that.

00:59:09.510 --> 00:59:12.150
You're coming from a
totally different place

00:59:12.150 --> 00:59:14.874
and you can't even talk
the talk, which again,

00:59:14.874 --> 00:59:18.750
you being able to talk the
talk, you hope that helps,

00:59:18.750 --> 00:59:21.180
helps them help you better.

00:59:21.180 --> 00:59:23.310
No one should have to go through that.

00:59:23.310 --> 00:59:25.350
Like this is, this is the part

00:59:25.350 --> 00:59:28.380
that makes me crazy, you know?

00:59:28.380 --> 00:59:32.370
But I really appreciate you
sharing that and I wish you

00:59:32.370 --> 00:59:33.820
and Marissa all the best

00:59:34.905 --> 00:59:38.350
and you know, I wish you
the best in your career

00:59:39.256 --> 00:59:40.556
as well as you go forward.

00:59:45.780 --> 00:59:49.828
Oh, well I pity those nurses
if they don't get it right.

00:59:49.828 --> 00:59:52.683
(group laughing)

00:59:52.683 --> 00:59:53.830
- That's why I said they
know me by the first name.

00:59:54.937 --> 00:59:57.687
(group laughing)

01:00:00.270 --> 01:00:01.113
- Anyone else?

01:00:02.640 --> 01:00:03.663
Don't be shy.

01:00:08.430 --> 01:00:10.440
- So I would like to thank you very much

01:00:10.440 --> 01:00:13.653
for your thought provoking, you know,

01:00:14.782 --> 01:00:17.310
experience that you shared with us.

01:00:17.310 --> 01:00:19.530
And also thank you, all of you

01:00:19.530 --> 01:00:21.810
for your very good questions.

01:00:21.810 --> 01:00:26.010
I just want to, you know,
reiterate what you all,

01:00:26.010 --> 01:00:27.960
you know, alluded to,
and I think that we need

01:00:27.960 --> 01:00:32.013
to really pause and thinking
about what you said, you know,

01:00:32.850 --> 01:00:37.850
that mortality rate, infant mortality rate

01:00:38.220 --> 01:00:40.860
and maternal mortality rates

01:00:40.860 --> 01:00:43.260
are raising in this
country that, you know,

01:00:43.260 --> 01:00:48.260
they are rising in this country
and they are preventable.

01:00:48.540 --> 01:00:50.970
Another thing that I wanted
to add to it that is,

01:00:50.970 --> 01:00:55.970
that makes it even more outrageous is that

01:00:56.250 --> 01:01:01.250
US spend almost five times more
in healthcare services than

01:01:01.410 --> 01:01:04.710
any other, you know,
high income countries.

01:01:04.710 --> 01:01:08.730
So it's, it's not only about, you know,

01:01:08.730 --> 01:01:10.740
how much money do we use in,

01:01:10.740 --> 01:01:12.780
to provide healthcare services.

01:01:12.780 --> 01:01:17.370
And the problem is that those, you know,

01:01:17.370 --> 01:01:22.290
high rates are not seen
in the white population.

01:01:22.290 --> 01:01:27.030
So that means that there is
nothing else that is, you know,

01:01:27.030 --> 01:01:31.020
causing this problem then
racism, nothing else.

01:01:31.020 --> 01:01:34.000
And we need to call it out
and we need to talk about it

01:01:34.911 --> 01:01:36.120
and we need to act on it

01:01:36.120 --> 01:01:39.420
because I think that that
is, I cannot, you know,

01:01:39.420 --> 01:01:43.230
it is outrageous for me to
just as a healthcare provider

01:01:43.230 --> 01:01:45.780
and healthcare educator to just see

01:01:45.780 --> 01:01:48.750
that happening year out and year in.

01:01:48.750 --> 01:01:51.120
And we cannot, you know, that not only

01:01:51.120 --> 01:01:52.710
that we cannot stop it,

01:01:52.710 --> 01:01:56.190
but it's, you know, increasing constantly.

01:01:56.190 --> 01:01:59.400
And that is so important
for our next generation

01:01:59.400 --> 01:02:02.520
of healthcare providers here, our midwives

01:02:02.520 --> 01:02:06.664
and other, you know, nurses
that we are graduating that

01:02:06.664 --> 01:02:10.410
really we need to become the advocate

01:02:10.410 --> 01:02:15.410
and we need to do something
about be being the voice of our,

01:02:15.932 --> 01:02:17.172
you know, the, the people who

01:02:17.172 --> 01:02:18.930
otherwise don't, don't have voice and that

01:02:18.930 --> 01:02:21.273
is just horrific.

01:02:22.380 --> 01:02:23.910
- Yeah, I appreciate that comment.

01:02:23.910 --> 01:02:27.990
We had, we had a scene that,
that we had to take out

01:02:27.990 --> 01:02:31.710
of the film that was a
group of midwives talking

01:02:31.710 --> 01:02:34.620
around a table about their experience.

01:02:34.620 --> 01:02:38.730
And one of the things, and
they are, they work in hospital

01:02:38.730 --> 01:02:41.970
and a couple of them were saying,

01:02:41.970 --> 01:02:44.070
one in particular said
they think of themselves

01:02:44.070 --> 01:02:46.230
as the guardian at the gate

01:02:46.230 --> 01:02:48.930
as being midwives in
the hospital system so

01:02:48.930 --> 01:02:53.743
that when they see behaviors that are

01:02:54.690 --> 01:02:57.750
racist or traumatic to
a patient, they will go

01:02:57.750 --> 01:03:00.194
and try to correct that behavior.

01:03:00.194 --> 01:03:02.430
I think you're a 100% right,

01:03:02.430 --> 01:03:03.873
we have to call it out.

01:03:04.830 --> 01:03:07.920
You know, the problem is that
those of us in this room,

01:03:07.920 --> 01:03:11.577
we may, you know, we, we, and
I believe in anti-racist work

01:03:11.577 --> 01:03:14.790
and I, but there are moments
when it's very hard for me,

01:03:14.790 --> 01:03:16.320
I get very cynical

01:03:16.320 --> 01:03:20.940
because I, you know, as this
country seems to be moving in,

01:03:20.940 --> 01:03:24.180
I don't know what this crazy
direction we're moving in,

01:03:24.180 --> 01:03:25.890
people don't seem to really care.

01:03:25.890 --> 01:03:28.020
They don't care about
Black people, straight up.

01:03:28.020 --> 01:03:30.273
They just don't care about Black people.

01:03:32.814 --> 01:03:33.690
And so I try to think of it like,

01:03:33.690 --> 01:03:34.680
and I often will say, well,

01:03:34.680 --> 01:03:36.780
even if you don't care about Black people,

01:03:36.780 --> 01:03:38.545
you should care about this issue

01:03:38.545 --> 01:03:40.773
because it's coming for you.

01:03:41.970 --> 01:03:45.870
You know, if you fix it early,
like in the, like the, like

01:03:45.870 --> 01:03:49.710
drug crisis, if in the '80's
instead of vilifying people

01:03:49.710 --> 01:03:51.907
because they had crack addictions

01:03:51.907 --> 01:03:53.126
and calling them crack
heads and locking them up

01:03:53.126 --> 01:03:56.670
and you know, treating them
poorly, maybe by the time we got

01:03:56.670 --> 01:04:00.360
to this opiate situation
we would be a little better

01:04:00.360 --> 01:04:03.150
prepared to deal with it, you know?

01:04:03.150 --> 01:04:05.010
But people don't learn.

01:04:05.010 --> 01:04:09.538
And again, white supremacy
is a very white supremacy.

01:04:09.538 --> 01:04:14.538
Our paternalistic society,
they're very powerful

01:04:14.550 --> 01:04:17.520
and people are not trying
to give it up, you know,

01:04:17.520 --> 01:04:20.250
but to your point, we
do have to call it out

01:04:20.250 --> 01:04:22.459
and we do have to fight as much as we can

01:04:22.459 --> 01:04:25.590
and we have to be slick and we have

01:04:25.590 --> 01:04:27.400
to call out people like Edward Bloom

01:04:27.400 --> 01:04:30.750
and we have to figure out
how we go up against him

01:04:30.750 --> 01:04:32.373
and his cohorts as well.

01:04:37.860 --> 01:04:41.115
- Thank you so much for the
film and for doing this today.

01:04:41.115 --> 01:04:46.115
I'm curious, the process

01:04:47.190 --> 01:04:51.210
of getting to midwifery and
in particular birth center

01:04:51.210 --> 01:04:54.360
and community-based midwifery
is such a theme in this,

01:04:54.360 --> 01:04:58.410
and whether that came out of
like Bruce and Amber's story

01:04:58.410 --> 01:05:00.420
because they had sought that out,

01:05:00.420 --> 01:05:03.540
or if it was sort of in
your mind to begin with

01:05:03.540 --> 01:05:06.480
because that's, you know,
the world that I'm in

01:05:06.480 --> 01:05:10.380
and I can see it very
clearly that a big part

01:05:10.380 --> 01:05:13.440
of the solution to this
is community-based care

01:05:13.440 --> 01:05:15.150
and community birth.

01:05:15.150 --> 01:05:17.940
But the reality is
that's a very white world

01:05:17.940 --> 01:05:20.550
and that's not what, you know,
Black people are accessing

01:05:20.550 --> 01:05:22.290
or even necessarily trying

01:05:22.290 --> 01:05:24.630
to access in a lot of
parts of the country.

01:05:24.630 --> 01:05:29.100
And so, you know, when I
advocate for, you know,

01:05:29.100 --> 01:05:31.320
community birth as part of
the solution to health equity,

01:05:31.320 --> 01:05:36.090
I often hear like, you know,
that's not necessarily true

01:05:36.090 --> 01:05:37.350
or how do you know, or whatever.

01:05:37.350 --> 01:05:38.970
So I guess I'm just asking
you, how did you know

01:05:38.970 --> 01:05:40.350
and how did that become such a,

01:05:40.350 --> 01:05:42.450
such a theme in the, in the film?

01:05:42.450 --> 01:05:43.470
- Yeah, I, I don't know.

01:05:43.470 --> 01:05:45.406
I feel like the, the Black folks in

01:05:45.406 --> 01:05:48.690
that I came across really
do advocate for that kind

01:05:48.690 --> 01:05:50.730
of community birthing.

01:05:50.730 --> 01:05:52.200
For us, you're right.

01:05:52.200 --> 01:05:56.160
With Bruce and Amber,
it was a midwife who saw

01:05:56.160 --> 01:05:59.490
that her platelets were
dropping, who sent her

01:05:59.490 --> 01:06:01.020
to actually to the hospital

01:06:01.020 --> 01:06:03.327
for care when she realized
she couldn't care for her.

01:06:03.327 --> 01:06:07.346
We also went down to
Tulsa, Oklahoma, as you saw

01:06:07.346 --> 01:06:10.770
with Neel Shah, and met up

01:06:10.770 --> 01:06:13.260
with a Tulsa Birth Equity Initiative,

01:06:13.260 --> 01:06:15.753
which is providing doula care down there.

01:06:17.250 --> 01:06:21.390
Also, separately, we had
heard about Helena Grant

01:06:21.390 --> 01:06:25.380
doing amazing work at
Woodhull Hospital as the head

01:06:25.380 --> 01:06:27.480
of the midwifery program there.

01:06:27.480 --> 01:06:30.000
And so, you know, I think just, you know,

01:06:30.000 --> 01:06:33.000
getting into the world, you
know, we were just hearing lots

01:06:33.000 --> 01:06:35.253
of different things that were going on.

01:06:37.608 --> 01:06:40.620
And I think, you know, I do
think that there, I mean,

01:06:40.620 --> 01:06:43.560
Shamony had a midwife
at one point. She did.

01:06:43.560 --> 01:06:47.151
And so we were hearing
about midwives talking

01:06:47.151 --> 01:06:51.720
to people about, you know,
I think we know this,

01:06:51.720 --> 01:06:53.387
we gotta save ourselves.

01:06:53.387 --> 01:06:56.426
And so the community is
trying to figure it out

01:06:56.426 --> 01:06:58.786
and everyone's talking.

01:06:58.786 --> 01:07:03.420
And then when we went down to
Tulsa, I mean, that was just,

01:07:03.420 --> 01:07:05.070
you know, meeting the people at Tulsa

01:07:05.070 --> 01:07:09.660
Birth Equity Initiative,
a doula program down there

01:07:09.660 --> 01:07:11.820
that connected us with
Felicia, who's the woman

01:07:11.820 --> 01:07:13.560
that gave birth in the film,

01:07:13.560 --> 01:07:15.150
who really thought she was gonna give

01:07:15.150 --> 01:07:16.860
birth for, with a doctor.

01:07:16.860 --> 01:07:19.350
We, and she just decided
at the last minute

01:07:19.350 --> 01:07:21.180
to do something else.

01:07:21.180 --> 01:07:26.010
Those women actually,
the, the, the, the head of

01:07:26.010 --> 01:07:29.640
that birthing center
actually is indigenous.

01:07:29.640 --> 01:07:34.410
And so, you know, the
community is really trying

01:07:34.410 --> 01:07:37.350
to do the work to protect themselves

01:07:37.350 --> 01:07:39.210
and come up with solutions to themselves.

01:07:39.210 --> 01:07:41.673
And I think that's how,
that's how we found them.

01:07:49.080 --> 01:07:52.080
Oh, and I do wanna say too,
Neel Shah, to his credit,

01:07:52.080 --> 01:07:55.080
the doctor in the film from
Harvard also has worked,

01:07:55.080 --> 01:07:58.830
works a lot with midwives and so,

01:07:58.830 --> 01:08:01.530
and I appreciate him and
I do wanna shout him out

01:08:01.530 --> 01:08:05.400
for this because as a
obstetrician gynecologist,

01:08:05.400 --> 01:08:09.991
he really is an advocate
for midwifery care.

01:08:09.991 --> 01:08:11.040
And I think that's really important

01:08:11.040 --> 01:08:15.750
because having doctors
who understand the role

01:08:15.750 --> 01:08:19.320
that midwives play, I
think is really critical.

01:08:19.320 --> 01:08:20.880
Understanding that it's not that,

01:08:20.880 --> 01:08:23.987
that everybody can work
together is really critical.

01:08:23.987 --> 01:08:27.363
And Neil was really, is
really fantastic in that way.

01:08:33.840 --> 01:08:35.340
- [Emcee] Any other questions?

01:08:36.360 --> 01:08:37.193
You sure?

01:08:57.658 --> 01:09:01.000
- (speaking faintly) Future generation

01:09:01.000 --> 01:09:01.833
to make credible changes

01:09:01.833 --> 01:09:05.880
and your work is contributing
so much to informing public

01:09:05.880 --> 01:09:10.629
and us about where we need
to go, what we need to do.

01:09:10.629 --> 01:09:14.087
So thank you for being
here and I appreciate it.

01:09:14.087 --> 01:09:14.920
Well, thank you for having me.

01:09:14.920 --> 01:09:16.620
And this is how we do it, right?

01:09:16.620 --> 01:09:19.350
Like, we come together, we
have these conversations.

01:09:19.350 --> 01:09:22.170
I know I feel full from this conversation,

01:09:22.170 --> 01:09:24.000
from all the work that you all are doing.

01:09:24.000 --> 01:09:25.830
And as I said, I take it with me and I,

01:09:25.830 --> 01:09:27.990
and I hope we all leave here

01:09:27.990 --> 01:09:30.167
and keep having conversations

01:09:30.167 --> 01:09:32.460
with each other, with other people.

01:09:32.460 --> 01:09:36.150
I will say the one thing,
you know, that I think is

01:09:36.150 --> 01:09:38.440
so important, and we've touched on here

01:09:39.720 --> 01:09:41.460
is a cultural shift in

01:09:41.460 --> 01:09:44.670
how we think about
birthing in this country.

01:09:44.670 --> 01:09:49.170
Birth is a sacred process.
It is a natural process.

01:09:49.170 --> 01:09:53.340
No one is sick. It is an amazing process.

01:09:53.340 --> 01:09:55.360
And when you birth a baby

01:09:57.422 --> 01:09:58.860
and you go through that labor,
as Felicia says in the film,

01:09:58.860 --> 01:10:01.140
it's the hardest thing she's ever done.

01:10:01.140 --> 01:10:03.450
She knows she can do anything.

01:10:03.450 --> 01:10:06.900
And that's why we're so oppressed in it.

01:10:06.900 --> 01:10:09.390
Because if women really, if we're able

01:10:09.390 --> 01:10:12.030
to realize our full power,
for those of us who choose

01:10:12.030 --> 01:10:14.760
to give birth, oh my god,
these men wouldn't know,

01:10:14.760 --> 01:10:16.830
wouldn't know what to do with us.

01:10:16.830 --> 01:10:19.890
So they're trying to keep us tamped down.

01:10:19.890 --> 01:10:22.590
But I think, I think we
all need to think about,

01:10:22.590 --> 01:10:25.260
and I know I'm talking, I'm
preaching to the choir here,

01:10:25.260 --> 01:10:28.710
but like how we talk about
birth, I mean, as you see,

01:10:28.710 --> 01:10:30.180
I don't use the word delivery.

01:10:30.180 --> 01:10:33.300
I, no one delivered, no
doctors delivered a baby.

01:10:33.300 --> 01:10:35.550
Women do the work. They birth a baby.

01:10:35.550 --> 01:10:38.004
A baby is birthed here.

01:10:38.004 --> 01:10:42.501
And I think if we can
think about birthing as,

01:10:42.501 --> 01:10:44.550
and again, it's all about choice.

01:10:44.550 --> 01:10:48.990
If I choose to give birth,
how I choose to give birth,

01:10:48.990 --> 01:10:52.490
where I choose to give birth,
a woman has a choice in all

01:10:52.490 --> 01:10:55.290
of these things, who I
choose to birth with.

01:10:55.290 --> 01:10:59.640
When, when you start getting
that kind of language around,

01:10:59.640 --> 01:11:03.387
when we start talking to
young people about, you know,

01:11:03.387 --> 01:11:06.540
and we should talk to young
people about birthing, like,

01:11:06.540 --> 01:11:09.120
I don't know why it's such
a mystery all the time.

01:11:09.120 --> 01:11:11.910
It's an amazing process.

01:11:11.910 --> 01:11:14.190
We all had to go through it to get here.

01:11:14.190 --> 01:11:16.921
And we've been doing it
since the times of ancient.

01:11:16.921 --> 01:11:18.150
It's amazing.

01:11:18.150 --> 01:11:21.510
So I think I'll just end
with saying like, I just,

01:11:21.510 --> 01:11:24.000
my hope is that, and not
necessarily this project

01:11:24.000 --> 01:11:27.210
that I mentioned before, but as we go on,

01:11:27.210 --> 01:11:31.860
that we really think about
birthing as the amazing, sacred,

01:11:31.860 --> 01:11:35.250
awesome thing that those of us who choose

01:11:35.250 --> 01:11:37.770
to do it can do it.

01:11:37.770 --> 01:11:39.450
And hopefully that that helps people

01:11:39.450 --> 01:11:41.050
change the way we do it as well.

01:11:43.322 --> 01:11:46.572
(audience applauding)

01:11:51.678 --> 01:11:53.734
- I would like to, once
again, thank you very much for

01:11:53.734 --> 01:11:57.330
your activism and
everything that you do to be

01:11:57.330 --> 01:11:59.490
the voice of voiceless.

01:11:59.490 --> 01:12:03.000
And and thank you very
much for coming here

01:12:03.000 --> 01:12:07.770
and I, we can talk about, you know,

01:12:07.770 --> 01:12:11.523
speak for everyone here that
I think that this was really,

01:12:13.106 --> 01:12:15.067
you know, pro thought provoking

01:12:15.067 --> 01:12:17.220
and I know that we are
leaving here with a lot

01:12:17.220 --> 01:12:20.613
of thoughts in our, you know,
mind to continue this work.

01:12:20.613 --> 01:12:22.803
It's very important. Thank you so much.

01:12:23.988 --> 01:12:25.267
(audience applauding)

01:12:25.267 --> 01:12:27.850
- Thank you so much. Thank you.

01:12:31.279 --> 01:12:33.862
(bright music)

